\textbf{Stable State.}
Upon the completion of core reduction for virtual machines, the scheduling module enters a stable state. In this state, the various metrics of both virtual machines and the system remain stable. When the scheduling module is in a stable state, no modifications to core affinity are made. Instead, it collects data from the monitoring module every second to assess whether the entire system remains in a stable state.

% The stable state continually monitors three key metrics:
% \textit{Scheduling latency of all virtual machine emulator threads. }Some virtual machines may not have undergone core reduction, and these can be influenced by the core reduction activities of other virtual machines, leading to an increase in their scheduling latency. In such cases, the stable state detects this abnormality and prompts the scheduling module to re-enter the core reduction state to adjust the core affinities of these virtual machines.\textit{CPU utilization of vCPU threads. } The CPU utilization of vCPU threads largely indicates the application's workload. Sudden changes in vCPU thread CPU utilization likely signify shifts in the virtual machine's load. When such changes occur, the scheduling module should promptly re-enter the core reduction state and reconfigure core affinities for these virtual machines.\textit{CPU utilization across the system. } An imbalance in CPU utilization suggests that the system may be in an unhealthy state. In this scenario, the scheduling module should revisit core affinities for the emulator threads of each virtual machine. When any of these three metrics deviate from their expected values, the scheduling module enters a oscillation state.

The stable state continually monitors three key metrics:
scheduling latency of all virtual machine emulator threads, CPU utilization of vCPU threads and CPU utilization across the system. The method for checking abnormal metric values involves examining whether the magnitude of the change exceeds a certain threshold. 

