\section{Conclusion}

In this study, we delve into the scheduling strategy of Emulator threads within virtual machine processes in a heterogeneous computing environment. We investigate the utilization of Emulator threads, the optimal number of core bindings, and the impact of their scheduling strategy on virtual machine performance. Based on a scheduling latency metric, a scheduler has been designed to dynamically adjust the core bindings of Emulator threads in response to the virtual machine's state. Experimental validation demonstrates that the scheduler effectively enhances virtual machine performance, with the maximum observed performance improvement being 40.7\%.

