\title{Game Theory in Social Media: A Stackelberg Model of Collaboration, Conflict, and Algorithmic Incentives} \author{By Arjan Khadka \\
	\texttt{arjank898@gmail.com}} \date{} 


\begin{document}

	\maketitle

	\begin{abstract} Social media platforms are ecosystems in which many decisions are constantly made for the benefit of the creators in order to maximize engagement, which leads to a maximization of income. The decisions, ranging from collaboration to public conflict or ``beefing,'' are heavily influenced by social media algorithms, viewer preferences, and sponsor risk. This paper models this interaction as a Stackelberg game in which the algorithm is the leader, setting exposure and reward rules, and the content creators are the followers, who optimize their content to maximize engagement. It focuses on two influencer strategies of collaborating and beefing. Viewer preferences are modeled indirectly through the algorithm's utility function, which rewards engagement metrics like click-through rate and watch time. Our simplified game-theoretic model demonstrates how different algorithmic priorities can shift creator strategies and provides insight into the equilibrium dynamics of social media influence. \end{abstract}

	\section{Introduction} Social media platforms such as TikTok, YouTube, and Instagram have transformed content
	creation into a high-stakes competition for user attention \cite{bakshy2015}. Creators
	now strategically compete to increase engagement, enhance visibility, and secure
	monetization opportunities \cite{goldfarb2011}. Every upload whether a friendly
	collaboration or a public “beef” is a deliberate tactic to maximize these outcomes
	\cite{ferrara2016}.

	A notable feature of this system is that creators do not act alone; they are influenced by three forces: \begin{itemize} \item \textbf{The Algorithm:} Determines what content is seen and rewarded. \item \textbf{The Viewers:} Their preferences shape algorithmic decisions. \item \textbf{The Sponsors:} Bring in revenue but are sensitive to brand safety and controversy. \end{itemize}

	This paper proposes a theoretical model using a Stackelberg game, a framework in which a leader makes a move first and the followers respond optimally. Here, the algorithm is the leader that chooses what kind of content to prioritize (e.g., favoring high watch-time or viral spikes). Creators are followers, responding to the algorithm's incentives by choosing content strategies such as collaboration or beefing. Viewer influence is embedded in the algorithm's utility function, as the algorithm promotes what viewers respond to.

	This paper makes the following contributions: \begin{itemize} \item Models creator behavior on social media as a simplified Stackelberg game. \item Defines strategic choices such as collaboration (mutual benefit) and beefing (competitive drama). \item Shows how the algorithm's reward structure affects the equilibrium behavior of creators. \end{itemize}

	\section{Model} To analyze strategic behavior on social media, we build a simplified theoretical model with four types of agents.

	\subsection{Players} \begin{itemize} \item \textbf{The Algorithm (Leader):} Sets exposure and reward rules by assigning weights to different engagement metrics, modeling algorithmic influence on user behavior \cite{tufekci2015, bakshy2015}. Optimizes content promotion to increase platform-wide engagement. \item \textbf{Content Creators (Followers):} Choose between strategies to maximize their own payoff: \begin{itemize} \item \emph{Collaboration:} Working with other creators to produce mutually beneficial content. \item \emph{Beefing:} Engaging in public conflict to attract attention. \end{itemize}
These strategies capture social media behaviors involving cooperation and conflict, which significantly affect engagement dynamics \cite{ferrara2016}.


	 \item \textbf{Viewers (Embedded into Algorithm's Utility):} Do not directly participate but influence the algorithm through their engagement behavior (clicks, watch time, shares). \item \textbf{Sponsors:} Provide external revenue to creators. Prefer brand-safe content and may penalize or withdraw support if creators engage in excessive drama \cite{goldfarb2011}.
	  \end{itemize}

	\subsection{Game Structure} The system is modeled as a Stackelberg game — a canonical leader-follower framework widely used to capture hierarchical strategic interactions \cite{stackelberg1934, gamebook} — with nested utility: \begin{enumerate} \item The algorithm chooses the reward structure by setting values for how much it rewards clicks, watch time, and shares. \item Creators observe this structure and choose their strategy (collab or beef) to maximize total reward. \item Viewer preferences are embedded into the algorithm's utility function. \item Sponsors apply a penalty to creators who engage in high-drama strategies like beefing. \end{enumerate}

	\subsection{Engagement Metrics and Weights} Let the algorithm assign weights: \begin{itemize} \item $\alpha$: importance of click-through rate \item $\beta$: importance of watch time \item $\gamma$: importance of shares \end{itemize}

	These engagement metrics are standard in platform analytics and directly influence content visibility and monetization \cite{goldfarb2011}.

	\vspace{0.5em}
	Each strategy produces different engagement outcomes:

\begin{table}[h!]
	\centering
	\begin{tabular}{|c|c|c|c|c|}
		\hline
		\textbf{Strategy} & \textbf{Clicks} & \textbf{Watch Time} & \textbf{Shares} & \textbf{Drama Risk} \\
		\hline
		Collaboration & 2 & 5 & 3 & 0 \\
		Beefing & 5 & 2 & 4 & 3 \\
		\hline
	\end{tabular}
	\caption{\textit{Note:} The values in the table below are illustrative and based on general trends observed on content platforms. Collaboration tends to yield longer watch times with moderate engagement, while beefing spikes clicks and shares but at the cost of higher controversy.
	}
\end{table}

	\subsection{Creator Utility Function (with Sponsor Penalty)} The creator wants to maximize engagement rewards and minimize sponsor loss. Their utility is defined as:

	\begin{equation} U_{\text{creator}}(s) = \alpha \cdot \text{Click}_s + \beta \cdot \text{Watch}_s + \gamma \cdot \text{Shares}_s - \delta \cdot \text{DramaRisk}_s \end{equation}

\textbf{Explanation of Variables:}
\begin{itemize}
	\item $s$: The strategy chosen by the creator. It can be either \textbf{Collaboration} or \textbf{Beefing}.

	\item $\alpha$: The weight or importance that the algorithm assigns to \textbf{click-through rate} (CTR). A higher $\alpha$ means the algorithm highly values content that gets clicked on often.

	\item $\beta$: The weight assigned to \textbf{watch time}. A higher $\beta$ means content that keeps viewers watching longer is prioritized.

	\item $\gamma$: The weight assigned to \textbf{shares}. Content that gets shared is rewarded more when $\gamma$ is high.

	\item $\delta$: The \textbf{sponsor sensitivity coefficient}. This measures how harshly sponsors punish creators for content that involves risk or controversy. A higher $\delta$ means sponsors are stricter, reducing incentives to beef.

	\item $\text{Click}_s, \text{Watch}_s, \text{Shares}_s$: The expected values of clicks, watch time, and shares generated by strategy $s$ (drawn from the table above).

	\item $\text{DramaRisk}_s$: A numeric value representing the amount of controversy or risk in strategy $s$. From the table, this is 0 for collaboration and 3 for beefing.
\end{itemize}

The creator selects the strategy $s$ that yields the highest $U_{\text{creator}}(s)$ given the weights set by the algorithm and penalties from sponsors.

\subsection{Nonlinear Utility Extension}

While the linear utility model offers tractability, real-world creator utility often exhibits nonlinear patterns due to diminishing returns and escalating penalties. We propose the following nonlinear utility function:

\[
U_{\text{creator}}(s) = \alpha \cdot \log(1 + \text{Click}_s) + \beta \cdot \sqrt{\text{Watch}_s} + \gamma \cdot \text{Shares}_s - \delta \cdot (\text{DramaRisk}_s)^2
\]

This formulation captures:

\begin{itemize}
	\item \textbf{Diminishing returns on clicks and watch time:} modeled via the logarithm and square root functions.
	\item \textbf{Nonlinear sponsor penalty:} the quadratic term penalizes high drama risk more heavily, reflecting sponsors' increasing aversion to controversy.
\end{itemize}

The nonlinear form allows for richer strategy dynamics and more realistic modeling of creator incentives.

\subsection{Algorithm Utility Function}

The algorithm's goal is to maximize overall viewer engagement on the platform. The algorithm’s utility function indirectly embeds viewer preferences through engagement metrics, reflecting how algorithmic curation adapts to user behavior \cite{bakshy2015, tufekci2015}. The algorithm's utility function is modeled as:

\begin{equation}
	U_{\text{algorithm}}(\alpha, \beta, \gamma) = \sum_{s \in \{\text{Collab}, \text{Beefing}\}} P_s \cdot (\alpha \cdot \text{Click}_s + \beta \cdot \text{Watch}_s + \gamma \cdot \text{Shares}_s)
\end{equation}

$P_s$: Proportion of creators choosing strategy $s$, derived from their best responses to $(\alpha, \beta, \gamma)$.


Here, the algorithm chooses the weights $\alpha$, $\beta$, and $\gamma$ to best align with viewer behavior and preferences. For example:

\begin{itemize}
	\item A viral, short-form platform like TikTok may prioritize \textbf{clicks} and \textbf{shares}, emphasizing rapid engagement and content virality.
	\item A long-form platform like YouTube may prioritize \textbf{watch time}, rewarding content that holds viewer attention for longer periods.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Strategy Flow}

The Stackelberg game unfolds in the following sequence:

\begin{enumerate}
	\item The \textbf{algorithm} selects the weights $\alpha$, $\beta$, and $\gamma$ based on viewer behavior and platform goals.
	\item The \textbf{content creators} observe these weights and compute which strategy—collaboration or beefing—maximizes their own utility according to the creator utility function.
	\item \textbf{Sponsors} impose penalties on creators who choose high-drama strategies, influencing creator choices.
	\item The combination of the algorithm's chosen weights and the creators' best responses form the \textbf{Stackelberg equilibrium}, representing the stable outcome of the interaction.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Equilibrium and Implications}

In this model, the \textbf{Stackelberg equilibrium} represents a stable outcome where neither the algorithm nor the creators want to change their choices because they are already optimizing their own goals, considering the other's behavior. This concept is widely used in hierarchical game theory to model leader-follower dynamics \cite{stackelberg1934, gamebook}, and has been applied in algorithmic decision-making and social media content optimization \cite{tang2019, hron2023modeling}.


\vspace{0.3cm}

\textbf{Step 1: Creators’ Best Response}

Given the algorithm’s choice of weights $\alpha$, $\beta$, and $\gamma$, each creator selects a strategy $s$ (either \textit{Collaboration} or \textit{Beefing}) to maximize their own utility function:

\[
U_{\text{creator}}(s) = \alpha \cdot \text{Click}_s + \beta \cdot \text{Watch}_s + \gamma \cdot \text{Shares}_s - \delta \cdot \text{DramaRisk}_s
\]

\begin{itemize}
	\item $\alpha$, $\beta$, $\gamma$: weights set by the algorithm, representing how important clicks, watch time, and shares are.
	\item $\text{Click}_s$, $\text{Watch}_s$, $\text{Shares}_s$: the engagement metrics resulting from strategy $s$.
	\item $\delta$: sponsor sensitivity coefficient (how much sponsors penalize drama).
	\item $\text{DramaRisk}_s$: risk level of the chosen strategy, 0 for Collaboration and positive for Beefing.
\end{itemize}

The creator’s best response $s^*$ is the strategy that yields the highest utility:

\[
s^*(\alpha, \beta, \gamma) = \arg \max_{s \in \{\text{Collaboration, Beefing}\}} U_{\text{creator}}(s)
\]

\vspace{0.3cm}

\textbf{Step 2: Algorithm’s Optimization}

The algorithm acts as a rational leader that anticipates the creators' best responses to its chosen weights. This hierarchical decision-making framework captures the interdependence between platform incentives and creator behavior \cite{tang2019, hron2023modeling}. The algorithm anticipates how creators will respond to the weights $(\alpha, \beta, \gamma)$ it chooses. Its goal is to maximize its own utility, which is a combination of engagement metrics:

\[
U_{\text{algorithm}}(\alpha, \beta, \gamma) = \alpha \cdot \text{Click}_{s^*} + \beta \cdot \text{Watch}_{s^*} + \gamma \cdot \text{Shares}_{s^*}
\]

Since the algorithm expects creators to choose the best strategy $s^*$ based on its weights, the algorithm's optimization problem is:

\[
(\alpha^*, \beta^*, \gamma^*) = \arg \max_{\alpha, \beta, \gamma} U_{\text{algorithm}}\big(s^*(\alpha, \beta, \gamma)\big)
\]

where $s^*(\alpha, \beta, \gamma)$ is the creators' best response given the weights.

\vspace{0.3cm}

\textbf{Summary:}

The \textit{creators} react to the \textit{algorithm’s} incentives by picking the strategy that maximizes their payoff.
The \textit{algorithm} anticipates the creators' reaction and chooses the weights to maximize overall engagement.
The interaction forms a \textit{Stackelberg game} where the leader (algorithm) moves first, and the followers (creators) respond optimally.
The equilibrium is reached when both the algorithm’s weights and creators' strategies are mutually optimal and stable.

\vspace{0.3cm}

\textbf{Implications of the Model:}

\begin{itemize}
	\item If the algorithm puts a high weight on clicks ($\alpha$) and shares ($\gamma$), creators will lean towards \textit{beefing} because this strategy yields more clicks and shares despite the sponsor penalty.
	\item If the algorithm emphasizes watch time ($\beta$), creators tend to prefer \textit{collaboration}, as it produces longer watch times and less drama.
	\item The sponsor penalty coefficient $\delta$ affects creators’ willingness to engage in drama-heavy content; a higher $\delta$ discourages beefing.
	\item This balance helps explain how platform algorithms shape the types of content that become popular and how sponsor concerns influence creator behavior. Such dynamics between platform incentives, creator strategies, and sponsor influence have been observed empirically in social media studies \cite{ferrara2016, goldfarb2011, tufekci2015}.

\end{itemize}

\section{Behavioral Evidence}

To ground this game-theoretic model in real-world phenomena, we examine behavioral evidence from empirical studies on creator behavior in digital ecosystems. Unlike models that assume fully rational agents, we incorporate the principle of \textit{bounded rationality}, which posits that individuals aim to make satisfactory decisions under constraints of limited information, cognitive processing capacity, and uncertainty. This framework better reflects the decision-making environment of social media creators, who must react dynamically to algorithmic opacity and fluctuating audience preferences.

\subsection{Satisficing and Heuristic-Based Decisions}

Creators often operate under conditions of incomplete feedback and limited foresight. Instead of globally optimal strategies, they employ satisficing behaviors—seeking "good enough" outcomes through trial-and-error and local optimizations.

\begin{itemize}
	\item \textbf{Adaptive Behavior:} Empirical studies show that creators frequently use simple heuristics, such as imitating trending formats or replicating previously successful videos \cite{choi2023creatorfriendly, verwiebe2024mercurial}. This aligns with bounded rationality, where decision rules are derived from experience rather than formal optimization.
	\item \textbf{Limited Lookahead:} In beef scenarios, creators often escalate conflict without anticipating long-term reputation damage. This is consistent with limited-depth strategic reasoning—akin to level-$k$ thinking in behavioral game theory \cite{camerer2004advances}.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Conflict as a Heuristic for Engagement}

Creators often exploit interpersonal conflict (``beefing'') as a cognitively simple yet effective engagement strategy.

\begin{itemize}
	\item \textbf{Availability and Salience Biases:} Conflict is highly salient and cognitively sticky, increasing the likelihood that creators will choose such strategies under uncertainty. Viewers disproportionately attend to conflictual content, making beef a high-reward heuristic.
	\item \textbf{Retaliatory Dynamics:} Behavioral experiments show that humans tend to respond to perceived slights with retaliation even when cooperation is payoff-dominant \cite{falk2006neural}. This helps explain why creators may escalate conflict cycles despite long-term costs.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Collaboration and Satisficing Coalitions}

Collaboration serves as a low-risk strategy that creators use when direct competition is costly or ineffective. These alliances are often formed not through exhaustive cost-benefit analysis but through local social cues and mutual interest.

\begin{itemize}
	\item \textbf{Rule-of-Thumb Strategies:} Cross-promotional collaborations often follow heuristics such as ``collab with creators of similar size'' or ``within the same niche'' \cite{zheng2023agency}. These satisficing rules bypass complex optimization and reflect bounded rational decision-making.
	\item \textbf{Reputation Spillover:} The decision to collaborate often considers the qualitative perception of the partner’s brand. Creators act under social bounded rationality, where social proof and prior exposure play a major role.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Algorithmic and Audience Feedback Loops}

Both the platform algorithm and viewers act as environment-level forces, shaping creators’ heuristics and reactive behaviors.

\begin{itemize}
	\item \textbf{Behavioral Adaptation to Algorithms:} Given limited transparency, creators treat the recommendation algorithm as a black box and adapt through experimental learning, A/B testing, and imitation \cite{covington2016deep}. This trial-and-error approach is a hallmark of bounded rationality.
	\item \textbf{Audience Influence:} Viewer feedback (likes, comments, shares) informs creators’ content adjustments. Studies show creators are highly reactive to negative feedback, often over-correcting or altering future strategies based on limited data \cite{kizilcec2021algorithms}.
\end{itemize}

These findings support our use of a game-theoretic model where players are not fully rational but instead behave strategically under cognitive and informational constraints. Bounded rationality offers a more psychologically plausible framework for understanding creator decisions in dynamic social media environments.


\section{Numerical Examples and Simulations}

To better understand how the model behaves under different conditions, we analyze numerical examples using specific parameter values. This helps illustrate how the algorithm's preferences and sponsor sensitivity influence creators' strategic choice \cite{bakshy2015,covington2016deep}.


\subsection{Parameters Setup}

Recall the algorithm assigns weights to engagement metrics:
\[
\alpha \quad \text{(weight on clicks)}, \quad \beta \quad \text{(weight on watch time)}, \quad \gamma \quad \text{(weight on shares)}.
\]
These weights represent how much the platform values each engagement metric when deciding which content to promote \cite{hron2023modeling,tang2019}.

Additionally, the parameter
\[
\delta
\]
represents the sponsor sensitivity coefficient. It quantifies how much creators are penalized for engaging in drama-heavy strategies, such as beefing, due to potential loss of sponsorship or brand safety concerns \cite{zheng2023agency}.

\subsection{Creator Utility Function Recap}

Each creator chooses a strategy \( s \in \{\text{Collaboration}, \text{Beefing}\} \) to maximize their utility:
\[
U_{\text{creator}}(s) = \alpha \cdot \text{Click}_s + \beta \cdot \text{Watch}_s + \gamma \cdot \text{Shares}_s - \delta \cdot \text{DramaRisk}_s,
\]
where:
\begin{itemize}
	\item \(\text{Click}_s, \text{Watch}_s, \text{Shares}_s\) are the engagement metrics generated by strategy \( s \).
	\item \(\text{DramaRisk}_s\) is zero for collaboration (a low-risk strategy) and positive for beefing (high-risk).
	\item The first three terms represent positive engagement rewards from the algorithm.
	\item The last term represents negative penalties from sponsors for drama.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Example 1: Baseline Parameters}

Suppose the platform values watch time most highly, reflecting a long-form content focus, and moderately values clicks and shares. Set:
\[
\alpha = 1.0, \quad \beta = 2.0, \quad \gamma = 1.5, \quad \delta = 1.0.
\]
\cite{covington2016deep}


Using the illustrative engagement metrics from Table 1:
\[
\begin{array}{c|cccc}
	\text{Strategy} & \text{Clicks} & \text{Watch Time} & \text{Shares} & \text{Drama Risk} \\
	\hline
	\text{Collaboration} & 2 & 5 & 3 & 0 \\
	\text{Beefing} & 5 & 2 & 4 & 3 \\
\end{array}
\]

Calculate creator utility for each strategy:

\paragraph{Collaboration:}
\[
U_{\text{creator}}(\text{Collab}) = 1.0 \times 2 + 2.0 \times 5 + 1.5 \times 3 - 1.0 \times 0 = 2 + 10 + 4.5 = 16.5.
\]
Explanation:
- The algorithm rewards 2 clicks, multiplied by \(\alpha=1.0\), contributing 2 units.
- Watch time is heavily rewarded (5 units \(\times \beta=2.0 = 10\)).
- Shares add \(3 \times 1.5 = 4.5\) units.
- No sponsor penalty applies (\(\text{DramaRisk} = 0\)).

\paragraph{Beefing:}
\[
U_{\text{creator}}(\text{Beef}) = 1.0 \times 5 + 2.0 \times 2 + 1.5 \times 4 - 1.0 \times 3 = 5 + 4 + 6 - 3 = 12.
\]
Explanation:
- More clicks (5 units) but less watch time (2 units).
- Shares contribute 6 units.
- A penalty of 3 units (due to drama risk multiplied by \(\delta=1.0\)) is subtracted.

\paragraph{Interpretation:}
Since \(16.5 > 12\), creators prefer collaboration under these weights because the platform's emphasis on watch time benefits collaboration more, and sponsor penalties reduce the attractiveness of beefing \cite{hron2023modeling,zheng2023agency}.

\subsection{Example 2: Increasing Sponsor Sensitivity}

Now increase the sponsor sensitivity to:
\[
\delta = 2.5,
\]
reflecting stricter brand safety concerns \cite{zheng2023agency}.

\paragraph{Collaboration:}
Utility remains:
\[
U_{\text{creator}}(\text{Collab}) = 16.5,
\]
since there is no drama penalty.

\paragraph{Beefing:}
\[
U_{\text{creator}}(\text{Beef}) = 5 + 4 + 6 - 2.5 \times 3 = 15 - 7.5 = 7.5.
\]

\paragraph{Interpretation:}
With stronger sponsor penalties, beefing utility decreases further, making collaboration even more favorable.

\subsection{Example 3: Algorithm Prioritizes Clicks and Shares}

Suppose the platform values clicks and shares more than watch time, as might be the case for a viral, short-form platform:
\[
\alpha = 2.5, \quad \beta = 0.5, \quad \gamma = 2.0, \quad \delta = 1.0.
\]
\cite{choi2023creatorfriendly}


Calculate utilities:

\paragraph{Collaboration:}
\[
U_{\text{creator}}(\text{Collab}) = 2.5 \times 2 + 0.5 \times 5 + 2.0 \times 3 - 1.0 \times 0 = 5 + 2.5 + 6 = 13.5.
\]

\paragraph{Beefing:}
\[
U_{\text{creator}}(\text{Beef}) = 2.5 \times 5 + 0.5 \times 2 + 2.0 \times 4 - 1.0 \times 3 = 12.5 + 1 + 8 - 3 = 18.5.
\]

\paragraph{Interpretation:}
Beefing yields higher utility here because the algorithm's reward structure heavily favors clicks and shares, which beefing generates more of. Despite the sponsor penalty, creators may prefer beefing to maximize exposure. \cite{verwiebe2024mercurial,zheng2023agency}

\subsection{Summary}

These examples demonstrate:
\begin{itemize}
	\item How the platform's priorities \((\alpha, \beta, \gamma)\) directly influence creator incentives.
	\item How sponsor sensitivity \(\delta\) serves as a deterrent for drama-heavy strategies.
	\item The interplay between maximizing engagement and managing brand risk, shaping the Stackelberg equilibrium.
\end{itemize}

This quantitative framework helps explain real-world social media dynamics, where platforms balancing different goals produce distinct creator behaviors. \cite{bakshy2015,hron2023modeling,tang2019}


\section{Policy and Design Implications}

The simulations and utility models reveal key leverage points in the social media ecosystem. The following implications are drawn from the Stackelberg structure of the game:

\subsection{Platform Incentive Design Matters}

Since the algorithm is the Stackelberg leader, its choice of weights \((\alpha, \beta, \gamma)\) determines the creators’ strategic responses \cite{covington2016deep,zheng2023agency}.
 Small changes in algorithmic priorities can dramatically shift creator behavior:

\begin{itemize}
	\item Emphasizing watch time (\(\beta\)) encourages thoughtful, long-form, and collaborative content \cite{choi2023creatorfriendly}.
	\item Overweighting clicks (\(\alpha\)) and shares (\(\gamma\)) can incentivize sensationalism or drama to provoke engagement spikes \cite{verwiebe2024mercurial}.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Design Implication:} Platforms can steer content quality by transparently adjusting how they rank and reward engagement metrics.

\subsection{Sponsor Pressure Can Be Modeled and Utilized}

The sponsor penalty parameter \(\delta\) captures external pressure against risky content. Higher values of \(\delta\) penalize drama and make collaboration more appealing.
\cite{zheng2023agency}\vspace{0.5em}

\textbf{Design Implication:} Platforms could internalize brand-safety risks into their algorithm, effectively increasing \(\delta\) in real-time, to prevent toxic content from rising.

\subsection{Strategic Transparency Reduces Manipulation}

If creators clearly understand the platform's algorithmic values (i.e., know \(\alpha, \beta, \gamma\)), they can optimize accordingly. While this increases efficiency, it may also encourage gaming the system. \cite{bakshy2015,falk2006neural}

\textbf{Design Implication:} Partial transparency—showing general priorities without disclosing exact formulas—can balance healthy strategic behavior with unpredictability to prevent manipulation.

\subsection{Viewer Preferences Are Indirect but Powerful}

Even though viewers are not modeled as active players, their preferences are embedded in how the algorithm assigns \(\alpha, \beta, \gamma\). Thus, viewer engagement data acts as an implicit leader influence. \cite{kizilcec2021algorithms}

\textbf{Design Implication:} Platforms must carefully analyze viewer metrics to distinguish between temporary spikes (e.g., viral beefs) and sustainable engagement (e.g., meaningful collaborations).

\subsection{Toward Healthier Equilibria}

The Stackelberg equilibrium changes as platform incentives and external penalties change. A toxic content environment is not inevitable; it is a function of poorly tuned incentives. \cite{camerer2004advances,hron2023modeling}

\textbf{Design Implication:} Algorithmic responsibility means designing utility functions that lead to socially beneficial equilibria. This may include:
\begin{itemize}
	\item Demoting drama-heavy content through dynamic penalty scaling.
	\item Boosting cross-collaborative efforts via reward multipliers.
	\item Monitoring and responding to emergent negative behavior patterns algorithmically.
\end{itemize}

\section{Case Studies}

To illustrate the practical relevance of the Stackelberg game model developed in this paper, we analyze prominent real-world examples of creator behavior on social media platforms. These cases demonstrate how creators strategically choose between collaboration and beefing, influenced by algorithmic incentives, viewer preferences, and sponsor sensitivities, as captured by the model.

\subsection{MrBeast’s Collaborative Strategy}

Jimmy Donaldson, popularly known as MrBeast, exemplifies the \emph{collaboration} strategy within our model’s framework.\cite{davies2024goodintent,choi2023creatorfriendly}
 MrBeast’s content is characterized by large-scale collaborations involving multiple creators, generous giveaways, and challenge-based videos that foster positive engagement.\cite{hron2023modeling}  This approach typically generates high watch time and shares, metrics which in our model are weighted by \(\beta\) and \(\gamma\), respectively.

The algorithm’s utility function
\[
U_{\text{algorithm}} = \alpha \cdot \text{Clicks} + \beta \cdot \text{Watch Time} + \gamma \cdot \text{Shares}
\]
is optimized when creators produce content that maximizes these values. MrBeast’s videos consistently achieve long watch durations and frequent shares, signaling strong viewer interest and satisfying the algorithm’s priorities.

From the creator’s perspective, the utility function
\[
U_{\text{creator}}(s) = \alpha \cdot \text{Click}_s + \beta \cdot \text{Watch}_s + \gamma \cdot \text{Shares}_s - \delta \cdot \text{DramaRisk}_s
\]
is maximized with low drama risk (\(\text{DramaRisk}_{\text{collab}} = 0\)), which minimizes the sponsor penalty term \(\delta \cdot \text{DramaRisk}_s\). Sponsors generally favor this type of brand-safe content, reinforcing financial incentives aligned with collaboration. \cite{choi2023creatorfriendly}

MrBeast’s success demonstrates a Stackelberg equilibrium where the algorithm (leader) sets weights \(\beta, \gamma\) high to encourage sustained engagement, and creators (followers) respond by choosing low-risk, collaborative content that maximizes both creator utility and sponsor satisfaction. This equilibrium promotes stable, long-term growth for creators and a healthy content ecosystem.

\subsection{Beefing and Public Conflicts on YouTube}

Conversely, the \emph{beefing} strategy represents creators engaging in public disputes or controversies to attract viewer attention quickly. This approach tends to generate higher click-through rates (\(\alpha\)) and shares (\(\gamma\)) due to the viral nature of drama and sensationalism, albeit often at the cost of watch time (\(\beta\)) and increased sponsor risk.

A well-known example is the feud between Logan Paul and KSI, two influential YouTubers whose back-and-forth conflicts involved diss tracks, public calls-outs, and highly publicized boxing matches \cite{verwiebe2024mercurial}. Their beefs led to massive spikes in clicks and shares, illustrating the appeal of high \(\alpha\) and \(\gamma\) engagement metrics to the algorithm.

However, this comes with a significant sponsor penalty. Sponsors, wary of brand safety and controversy, may decrease ad spending or withdraw endorsements in response to drama-heavy content \cite{zheng2023agency}. This penalty is represented by the term \(\delta \cdot \text{DramaRisk}_s\) in the creator’s utility. For beefing, \(\text{DramaRisk}_{\text{beef}} > 0\), reflecting increased sponsor sensitivity.

The interplay of these forces determines creator strategy. If the algorithm prioritizes clicks and shares heavily (high \(\alpha, \gamma\)) and sponsor sensitivity \(\delta\) is low or sponsors tolerate controversy, creators are incentivized to beef for short-term gains. If sponsors impose strong penalties (\(\delta\) large), creators may avoid beefing despite algorithmic rewards.

This dynamic highlights how the Stackelberg model captures tension between algorithmic rewards and sponsor risks, producing a spectrum of equilibria depending on parameter values. Platforms seeking to curb harmful drama can influence \(\delta\) and adjust \(\alpha, \beta, \gamma\) to promote brand-safe content.

\subsection{General Observations on YouTube Beefing}

Beyond the Logan Paul–KSI feud, YouTube has witnessed numerous instances of beefing that underscore the model’s insights \cite{burgess2018youtube}. These conflicts typically cause viral surges in engagement metrics favored by algorithms but often lead to fluctuating sponsorship deals and sometimes platform intervention.

Creators engaging in beefing may do so strategically, weighing the immediate boost in clicks and shares against the risk of alienating sponsors or viewers preferring less dramatic content. This cost-benefit analysis aligns closely with our creator utility function, where \(\delta\) quantifies the sponsor risk aversion.

The model also suggests potential for feedback loops: viral beefing may reinforce algorithm weights on clicks and shares (\(\alpha, \gamma\)) as platforms capitalize on engagement spikes, which in turn further incentivizes beefing, creating challenges for platform governance and content moderation. \cite{tang2019}

\subsection{Broader Implications and Platform Governance}

These case studies reveal critical trade-offs for platform designers and regulators:

\begin{itemize}
	\item \textbf{Incentivizing Collaboration:} Platforms that prioritize watch time (\(\beta\)) and shares (\(\gamma\)) while imposing substantial penalties for drama (\(\delta\)) can foster a collaborative equilibrium, promoting sustainable, brand-safe content growth.
	\item \textbf{Managing Beefing Dynamics:} Platforms favoring click-driven engagement (\(\alpha\)) with weak sponsor penalties may unintentionally encourage drama, risking reputational harm and advertiser withdrawals.
	\item \textbf{Sponsor Role:} Sponsors act as external regulators, and their sensitivity (\(\delta\)) is pivotal in shaping creator strategies, highlighting the importance of aligning platform incentives with advertiser preferences. \cite{falk2006neural}

\end{itemize}

By formalizing these interactions within a Stackelberg framework, the model provides a foundation for designing algorithmic policies and sponsorship frameworks that balance engagement maximization with content quality and brand safety.

\bigskip

Overall, these real-world examples not only validate the theoretical model’s assumptions and structure but also demonstrate its potential to guide empirical analyses and inform platform policy decisions aiming to cultivate healthier and more sustainable social media ecosystems.

\section{Limitations and Future Work}

While our simplified Stackelberg model provides insight into how algorithmic incentives shape content creator behavior, it is important to acknowledge the model’s limitations and suggest avenues for refinement.

\subsection{Model Limitations}

\begin{itemize}
	\item \textbf{Binary Strategy Set:} The model reduces creator choices to two discrete strategies: collaboration and beefing \cite{krishna2009game}. In practice, content strategies exist on a continuum ranging from wholesome partnerships to subtle shade to overt controversy. This binary simplification limits the model’s ability to capture marginal or hybrid tactics (e.g., humorous parody, indirect responses, or temporary alliances). A continuous or multi-strategy framework could better model the complexity of real-world behavior.

	\item \textbf{Static Environment:} The current model treats the game as a one-shot Stackelberg interaction \cite{fudenberg1991game}. However, social media engagement is inherently dynamic. Viewer preferences evolve, algorithms adapt via machine learning, and creators update their strategies based on past feedback. Thus, a more realistic model would consider a dynamic Stackelberg game or even a reinforcement learning setting where the algorithm updates its weights over time based on observed engagement patterns.

	\item \textbf{Homogeneous Agents:} We assume all creators are identical in their utility functions and engagement outcomes \cite{gibbons1992primer}. In reality, creators differ in follower count, brand image, audience demographics, and risk tolerance. For instance, a controversial influencer may be rewarded for beefing, while a family-friendly creator may suffer severe losses. Modeling a population of heterogeneous creators could allow the emergence of mixed strategies and more nuanced equilibria.

	\item \textbf{Linear Utility and Penalty Functions:} The creator utility and sponsor penalty are modeled linearly \cite{mas1995microeconomic}:
	\[
	U_{\text{creator}}(s) = \alpha \cdot \text{Click}_s + \beta \cdot \text{Watch}_s + \gamma \cdot \text{Shares}_s - \delta \cdot \text{DramaRisk}_s
	\]
	However, sponsor reactions may be nonlinear—tolerating low levels of drama but imposing steep penalties past a certain threshold (e.g., a Heaviside step function). Likewise, engagement metrics may exhibit diminishing returns (e.g., logarithmic growth in utility). Incorporating nonlinear terms or utility curvature would improve realism.

	\item \textbf{No Direct Viewer or Sponsor Strategy:} Both viewers and sponsors are modeled passively \cite{young2015strategic}. The algorithm internalizes viewer behavior into weights $(\alpha, \beta, \gamma)$, and sponsors impose a fixed penalty. This omits strategic behavior like viewer backlash, boycott campaigns, or sponsors withdrawing mid-campaign. Modeling viewers and sponsors as active agents in a larger multi-agent game could add important feedback loops.

	\item \textbf{Single-Platform Focus:} The model assumes creators are only optimizing for one algorithm \cite{liu2020cross}. In reality, creators often produce content across multiple platforms simultaneously (e.g., reposting TikToks to Instagram Reels and YouTube Shorts), each with different reward systems. These cross-platform trade-offs are a major factor in real content strategy.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Directions for Future Work}

To enhance the model’s predictive power and applicability, we propose the following research directions:

\begin{itemize}
	\item \textbf{Multi-Strategy Extension:} Introduce a richer strategy set with continuous variables (e.g., drama level $d \in [0,1]$), or additional tactics like trending content, education, or satire. This allows creators to blend strategies and may lead to interior equilibria rather than strict binaries.

	\item \textbf{Repeated and Dynamic Games:} Extend the Stackelberg model to a repeated framework with time-evolving parameters. The algorithm can adjust weights $(\alpha_t, \beta_t, \gamma_t)$ based on observed viewer responses, while creators update strategies using best-response dynamics or reinforcement learning. This would better model long-term dynamics such as algorithm updates or changing trends.

	\item \textbf{Agent Heterogeneity and Network Effects:} Model creators as nodes in a network with varying attributes. Include influence graphs to represent who collaborates or beefs with whom. This opens the door to simulating virality and cascading drama, where one conflict triggers a wave of engagement through the network.

	\item \textbf{Empirical Calibration:} Collect engagement data from real platforms (e.g., using public APIs or scraped statistics) to estimate plausible values for weights and strategy payoffs. This would allow empirical testing of model predictions and alignment with real phenomena like algorithm changes or influencer controversies.

	\item \textbf{Cross-Platform Optimization:} Develop a multi-platform Stackelberg model where each algorithm sets different reward rules, and creators must choose strategies that balance competing incentives. This would mirror real-world creator behavior where maximizing engagement requires navigating divergent ecosystems.
\end{itemize}

\section{Conclusion}

In this paper, we proposed a game-theoretic model of social media interaction by framing content creator behavior as a Stackelberg game. We positioned the platform algorithm as the leader that sets engagement-based incentives, while creators are followers who strategically choose between collaboration and beefing based on the resulting payoffs.

The model captures how algorithmic priorities—represented by the weights $(\alpha, \beta, \gamma)$ on clicks, watch time, and shares—determine the strategic landscape. A central insight is that small shifts in these weights can lead to dramatic shifts in equilibrium behavior. For instance, if $\alpha$ (clicks) and $\gamma$ (shares) are emphasized, creators are incentivized to adopt high-drama strategies like beefing, even at the cost of watch time or sponsor loss. Conversely, a high $\beta$ (watch time) or a strong sponsor penalty $\delta$ can tip the balance toward more cooperative and brand-safe strategies like collaboration.

We formalized creator utility as:
\[
U_{\text{creator}}(s) = \alpha \cdot \text{Click}_s + \beta \cdot \text{Watch}_s + \gamma \cdot \text{Shares}_s - \delta \cdot \text{DramaRisk}_s
\]
and examined how this leads to a Stackelberg equilibrium, where the algorithm indirectly controls creator behavior by tuning these parameters.

Our numerical simulations demonstrated that:
\begin{itemize}
	\item Creator strategies are highly sensitive to the algorithm’s reward structure.
	\item Sponsor penalties act as a regulatory force that can suppress exploitative or controversial tactics.
	\item Viewer preferences, though not modeled as explicit players, are crucially embedded in the algorithm’s optimization objective.
\end{itemize}

Overall, this work provides a simplified but insightful lens into the strategic nature of social media ecosystems. It also raises important questions about the responsibility of platforms in shaping online discourse through algorithmic design. Future research could expand this model into dynamic, multi-agent, or empirically calibrated directions to better understand real-world creator behavior and platform governance.

