\subsection{Multi-dimensional example}
\label{sb:MultiD}
We now consider a discrete dynamical system that can be generalized to multiple dimensions in both $\mathbf{x}$ and $\mathbf{y}$.~Specifically, we examine the system:
\begin{align}
    \mathbf{\hat{x}}(k+1) &= \cos(\mathbf{\hat{B}} \mathbf{\hat{x}}(k)) + \mathbf{\hat{C}} \mathbf{\hat{y}}(k), \nonumber \\
    \mathbf{\hat{y}}(k+1) &= \sin(\mathbf{\hat{A}}\mathbf{\hat{y}}(k)),
    \label{eq:TP3_DS}
\end{align}
where  $\mathbf{\hat{x}} \in \mathbb{R}^N $, $\mathbf{\hat{y}} \in \mathbb{R}^M$, and $\mathbf{\hat{A}}\in\mathbb{R}^{M\times M}$, $\mathbf{\hat{B}}\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times N}$ and $\mathbf{\hat{C}}\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times M}$.~The equilibrium points of this system, say $(\mathbf{\hat{x}}_0,\mathbf{\hat{y}}_0)$, satisfy:
\begin{equation*}
    \mathbf{\hat{x}}_0 - \cos(\mathbf{\hat{B}}\mathbf{\hat{x}}_0) - \mathbf{\hat{C}} \mathbf{\hat{y}}_0 = \mathbf{0}_N, \qquad \mathbf{\hat{y}}_0 - \sin(\mathbf{\hat{A}}\mathbf{\hat{y}}_0) = \mathbf{0}_M. 
\end{equation*}
Here, we compute the IM around the origin $\mathbf{\hat{y}}_0=\mathbf{0}^M$, which implies that $\mathbf{\hat{x}}_0$ satisfies $\mathbf{\hat{x}}_0-\cos(\mathbf{\hat{B}}\mathbf{\hat{x}}_0)=\mathbf{0}^N$.~The Jacobian matrix of the system in \cref{eq:TP3_DS} at the equilibrium points $(\mathbf{\hat{x}}_0,\mathbf{\hat{y}}_0)$ is:
\begin{equation}
    \mathbf{J}(\mathbf{\hat{x}}_{0},\mathbf{\hat{y}}_{0}) = \begin{bmatrix}
        -diag \left( \sin(\mathbf{\hat{B}} \mathbf{\hat{x}}_0)\right)\mathbf{\hat{B}} & \mathbf{\hat{C}} \\
        \mathbf{0}^{M\times N} & diag \left( \cos(\mathbf{\hat{A}} \mathbf{\hat{y}}_0)\right) \mathbf{\hat{A}}
    \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix}
        -diag \left( \mathbf{1}^N- \mathbf{\hat{x}}_0^2\right)^{1/2} \mathbf{\hat{B}} & \mathbf{\hat{C}} \\
        \mathbf{0}^{M\times N} & \mathbf{\hat{A}}
    \end{bmatrix},
    \label{eq:Jac_eq_tp}
\end{equation}
where $\mathbf{0}^{M\times N}$ is the $M\times N$ zero matrix, $\mathbf{1}^N$ is the $N$-dim. column vector of ones and $\mathbf{\hat{x}}_0^2$ denotes the element-wise square of $\mathbf{\hat{x}}_0$.~\Cref{eq:Jac_eq_tp} implies that the stability of the equilibrium points $(\mathbf{\hat{x}}_0,\mathbf{\hat{y}}_0)$ depends on the eigenvalues of $\mathbf{\hat{B}}$ and $\mathbf{\hat{A}}$, since the eigenvalues of $-diag \left( \mathbf{1}_N- \mathbf{\hat{x}}_0^2\right)^{1/2}$ are always less than 1.

Next, we introduce deviation variables from the above equilibrium points $\mathbf{x} = \mathbf{\hat{x}}-\mathbf{\hat{x}}_{0}$ and $\mathbf{y} = \mathbf{\hat{y}}$, rewriting the system in \cref{eq:TP3_DS} in the form of \cref{eq:genDDS1} as:
\begin{align*}
    \mathbf{x}(k+1) &= diag(\mathbf{\hat{x}}_0) \cos(\mathbf{\hat{B}} \mathbf{x}(k)) - diag \left( \mathbf{1}^N- \mathbf{\hat{x}}_0^2\right)^{1/2} \sin(\mathbf{\hat{B}} \mathbf{x}(k)) + \mathbf{\hat{C}} \mathbf{y}(k) - \mathbf{\hat{x}}_0, \nonumber \\
    \mathbf{y}(k+1) &= \sin(\mathbf{\hat{A}}\mathbf{y}(k)),
    %\label{eq:DS_tp3eq1}
\end{align*}
with the equilibrium points now represented by the origin $(\mathbf{x}_0,\mathbf{y}_0)=(\mathbf{0}^N,\mathbf{0}^M)$ in the new state variables.~This system can be rewritten in the form of \cref{eq:genDDS2} by setting:
\begin{align}
\mathbf{A} & = \mathbf{\hat{A}}, \quad \mathbf{B} = - diag \left( \mathbf{1}^N- \mathbf{\hat{x}}_0^2\right)^{1/2} \mathbf{\hat{B}}, \quad 
\mathbf{C} = \mathbf{\hat{C}}, \nonumber \\
\mathbf{f}(\mathbf{x}(k),\mathbf{y}(k)) & =   diag(\mathbf{\hat{x}}_0) \left( \cos(\mathbf{\hat{B}} \mathbf{x}(k)) - \mathbf{1}^N \right) + diag \left( \mathbf{1}^N- \mathbf{\hat{x}}_0^2\right)^{1/2} \left(\mathbf{\hat{B}} \mathbf{x}(k)-\sin(\mathbf{\hat{B}} \mathbf{x}(k)) \right),
    \nonumber \\ 
\mathbf{g}(\mathbf{x}(k),\mathbf{y}(k)) & = \sin(\mathbf{\hat{A}}\mathbf{y}(k)) - \mathbf{\hat{A}}\mathbf{y}(k). \label{eq:TP3_DDSeq}
\end{align}
For any choise of matrices $\mathbf{\hat{A}}$, $\mathbf{\hat{B}}$ and $\mathbf{\hat{C}}$, \cref{eq:TP3_DDSeq} implies $\mathbf{f}(\mathbf{x}_0,\mathbf{y}_0) = \mathbf{g}(\mathbf{y}_0) = \mathbf{0}$ and $\partial \mathbf{f}/\partial \mathbf{x}\rvert_{(\mathbf{x}_0,\mathbf{y}_0)} = \partial \mathbf{f}/\partial \mathbf{y}\rvert_{(\mathbf{x}_0,\mathbf{y}_0)} = \partial \mathbf{g}/\partial \mathbf{y}\rvert_{(\mathbf{y}_0)} = \mathbf{0}$ with appropriate dimensionality.~However, to satisfy \Cref{ass1} the matrix $\mathbf{\hat{A}}$ needs to have non-zero eigenvalues.

In the sequel, we consider three cases of different dimensions for the original system in \cref{eq:TP3_DS} aligned with the above state space representation \cref{eq:TP3_DDSeq}.~We chose parameters such that the resulting system trajectories are attracted to their equilibrium points and the non-resonance conditions are satisfied, thus ensuring the existence of an IM through \Cref{th:th1Kaz}.~In all cases, we compute the PSE approximations $\tilde{\mathbf{x}}=\tilde{\boldsymbol{\pi}}^{PSE}(\mathbf{y})$ of the IM functionals by determining the coefficients of the Taylor series expansion in \cref{eq:IM_PSexp}, as described in \cref{app:IM_PS}.~The resulting PSE approximations of the IM are used for comparison with the IM approximations provided by the PI schemes.

\subsubsection{An example of $N=1$ and $M=1$}
\label{sbsb:N1M1}
First, we consider a simple case with $N=1$ and $M=1$; i.e., $\mathbf{x}\in\mathbb{R}$ and $\mathbf{y}\in\mathbb{R}$.~We select the parameter values $\mathbf{\hat{A}}=0.99$, $\mathbf{\hat{B}}=0.8$, $\mathbf{\hat{C}}=0.5$, which result to $\mathbf{\hat{x}}_0=0.8014$.~Substituting these values into the expressions in \cref{eq:TP3_DDSeq} shows that the resulting system satisfies the non-resonance condition of \Cref{th:th1Kaz}, since the eigenvalue of $\mathbf{\hat{A}}$, $k_1=0.99$, and the eigenvalue of $\mathbf{\hat{B}}$, $\lambda_1=-0.1196$, are not related by $k_1^d \neq \lambda_1$ for any $d\in \mathbb{N}$.~Hence, according to \Cref{th:th1Kaz}, a $N=1$-dim. IM $\mathcal{M}$ exists, with the mapping $\tilde{\mathbf{x}}=\tilde{\boldsymbol{\pi}}(\mathbf{y})$, where $\boldsymbol{\pi}:\mathbb{R}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}$, satisfying the NFEs in \cref{eq:NFEs_IM}.~Here, the IM develops around a stable fixed point, since $\lvert k_1\rvert,\lvert\lambda_1\rvert<1$.~The PSE approximation of the IM was computed up to degree $h=5$.

\subsubsection{An example of $N=1$ and $M=2$}
\label{sbsb:N1M2}
Next, we consider a case where $N=1$ but the input dynamics is realized by $M=2$ independent dynamic variables, that is $\mathbf{x} \in\mathbb{R}$ and $\mathbf{y}\in\mathbb{R}^2$.~We select the parameter values: 
\begin{equation}
    \mathbf{\hat{A}} = \begin{bmatrix}
        0.99 & 0.8 \\ -0.1 & 0.9 
    \end{bmatrix}, \qquad \mathbf{\hat{B}}=0.2, \qquad \mathbf{\hat{C}}=\begin{bmatrix}
        0.5 & -0.8
    \end{bmatrix},
\end{equation}
which result to $\mathbf{\hat{x}}_0=0.9808$.~For these parameter values, we obtain by \cref{eq:TP3_DDSeq} that the matrix $\mathbf{A}$ has a pair of complex eigenvalues of   $k_{1,2}=0.945\pm0.2792i$, while the eigenvalue of $\mathbf{\hat{B}}$ is $\lambda_1=-0.039$.~There is no resonance relation $k_1^{d_1}k_2^{d_2}=\lambda_1$ that holds true for any $d_1,d_2\in\mathbb{N}$ with $d_1+d_2>0$.~Since all the assumptions of \Cref{th:th1Kaz} are satisfied, a $N=1$-dim. IM $\mathcal{M}$ exists.~In this case however, the mapping $\tilde{\mathbf{x}}=\tilde{\boldsymbol{\pi}}(\mathbf{y})$ has two independent input variables, since $M=2$, i.e., $\boldsymbol{\pi}:\mathbb{R}^2\rightarrow\mathbb{R}$.~Here, the PSE approximation of the IM was computed up to $h=4$ degree.~Another important difference with the IM in \Cref{sbsb:N1M1} is that in this case, the IM is developed around a stable spiral, since $k_{1,2}$ are complex and $\lvert \mathfrak{Re}(k_{1,2})\rvert,\lvert\lambda_1
\rvert<1$.


\subsubsection{An example of $N=2$ and $M=3$}
\label{sbsb:N2M3}
Finally, we consider a case of the system in \cref{eq:TP3_DS} where $N=2$ and $M=3$.~In particular, we select the parameter values:
\begin{equation}
    \mathbf{\hat{A}} = \begin{bmatrix}
        0.9 & 0.8 & 0 \\ -0.1 & 0.7 & 0.4 \\ 0 & -0.5 & 0.8 
    \end{bmatrix}, \qquad \mathbf{\hat{B}}=\begin{bmatrix}
        -0.8 & 0.3 \\ 0.7 & -0.9
    \end{bmatrix}, \qquad \mathbf{\hat{C}}=\begin{bmatrix}
        0.2 & -0.4 & 0.7 \\ -0.1 & 0.8 & 0.9
    \end{bmatrix},
\end{equation}
which result to $\hat{\mathbf{x}}_0=[0.9072,~0.9715]^\top$.~Substituting these parameter values into \cref{eq:TP3_DDSeq}, implies that the matrix $\mathbf{A}$ has the eigenvalues $k_1=0.8727$, $k_{2,3}=0.7637\pm0.5234i$, while the matrix $\mathbf{B}$ has the eigenvalues $\lambda_1=0.4323$, $\lambda_2=0.1177$.~As in \cref{sbsb:N1M2}, these eigenvalues imply that the origin is a stable spiral.~Since no resonance condition exists for these eigenvalues (that is, there are no $d_1,d_2,d_3\in\mathbb{N}$ with $d_1+d_2+d_3>0$, such that $k_1^{d_1}k_2^{d_2}k_3^{d_3}=\lambda_1$ or $k_1^{d_1}k_2^{d_2}k_3^{d_3}=\lambda_2$), all the assumptions of \Cref{th:th1Kaz} are satisfied.~Hence, a $N=2$-dim. IM $\mathcal{M}$ develops around the origin, with the mapping $\tilde{\mathbf{x}}=\tilde{\boldsymbol{\pi}}(\mathbf{y})$, which in this case has $M=3$ inputs and $N=2$ outputs, that is $\boldsymbol{\pi}:\mathbb{R}^3\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^2$.~This IM mapping satisfies again the NFEs in \cref{eq:NFEs_IM} and for its PSE approximation we considered a multi-variate Taylor series expansion of degree $h=3$.    


\section{Numerical Results}
\label{sec:NumR}
This section evaluates the efficiency of the proposed PI hybrid scheme in learning IM approximations for the three examples described in \Cref{sec:Bench}.~We examine their convergence, numerical approximation accuracy and the computational training cost.~Additionally, we compare the IM approximations provided by the PI hybrid schemes with those obtained by purely using PI NNs and the PSE expansions derived by the symbolic approach proposed in \cite{kazantzis2001invariant,kazantzis2005model}.

Training and evaluation of the PI schemes followed the procedures outlined in \Cref{sbsb:train,sbsb:numAc}.~All simulations were carried out with a CPU Intel(R) Core(TM) CPU i7-13700H @ 2.40GHz, RAM 32.0 GB using MATLAB R2024b.

\subsection{Example with analytic invariant manifold}
\label{sb:res_ln}
We first consider the discrete dynamical system in \cref{eq:TP_DDS2}, for which the true IM mapping is known in \cref{eq:lnIM_anal}.~This allows us to compare the IM approximations with the exact IM.

The system in \cref{eq:TP_DDS2} is defined for $y\ge1$ (when $\beta<0$) and exhibits a singularity at $y=-1$.~We approximate the IM in the domain $\Omega=[-0.9,2]$.~For the PI hybrid scheme, we considered polynomials of degree $h=10$ and $h=20$, and NNs with $L=10$ neurons in the hidden layer.~The resulting number of parameters to be computed for the NN component is 31.~Accordingly, we collected $Q=31\times20=620$ collocation points to train the PI hybrid and pure NN schemes, uniformly sampled from $\Omega$.

Using these collocation points in $\Omega=[-0.9,2]$, we trained the PI hybrid schemes with power, Legendre and Chebyshev polynomials and $r=1$ (denoted as HPS, HLS and HCS $r=1$, respectively) and the PI NN scheme.~To explore the effect of the radius of convergence, we also trained the PI hybrid scheme with power series using $r=0.5$ (denoted as PI HPS $r=0.5$).~To examine the impact of polynomial degree, we considerd all hybrid schemes with $h=10$ (baseline) and $h=20$.~The convergence results of all 9 trained PI approximations are shown in \Cref{tb:conv_lnEx}, as obtained over 100 training realizations with different initial parameters; see \Cref{sbsb:train} for details.
