\section{Introduction}



Vulnerability detection aims to identify weaknesses in software systems to prevent potential security attacks and privacy leakage. Although tremendous and long-lasting efforts have been invested to construct vulnerability detection datasets~\cite{zhou2019devign,chen2023diversevul,nikitopoulos2021crossvul,bhandari2021cvefixes,fan2020ac,chakraborty2021deep,ding2024vulnerability}, existing datasets still suffer from quality issues, which significantly hindering the development of advanced large machine learning models. For instance, Ding et
al.~\cite{ding2024vulnerability} found that existing datasets suffer from low label accuracy and high duplicate rates, which lead to unreliable model performance. Chakraborty et al.~\cite{chakraborty2024revisiting} observed that current studies on vulnerability detection rely on datasets that are based on various criteria and may not reflect reality accurately. Chen et al.~\cite{chen2023diversevul} pointed out the label noise in current vulnerability detection datasets may introduce errors into the measurement of the model's performance.

Efforts to improve dataset quality typically involve collecting larger and more diverse datasets or refining annotation guidelines to minimize labeling errors~\cite{chakraborty2024revisiting,ding2024vulnerability}. As shown in {\S}2, our preliminary experiment demonstrated that training a detection model on partial (e.g., 70\%) data can outperform the one trained on the whole dataset. In other words, increasing the size of the training set does not always improve performance.
If more data is not useful, perhaps a better approach is to collect less data, but collect data in a smarter manner. 

Our key hypothesis is that: 

\begin{quote}
{\em ``Bad seeds'' in vulnerability detection datasets are often the examples that models find difficult to learn.}\end{quote}

To say that another way, the problem is not the size of the data set but the number of distracting examples.
This leads to the prediction, to be tested here, that:

\begin{quote}
{\em Identifying, then mitigating, the bad seeds can 
%provide valuable insights into dataset quality and 
improve model training efficiency. }
\end{quote}

To validate our hypothesis, we develop a dataset maps-based method to identify bad seeds and then adopt it into an {\em active learning} framework which is often considered as a practical solution for vulnerability detection, as collecting high-quality labels is extremely expensive.
Active learners reflect on the models built so far to decide what to classify next.
We first employ our method to enable us to categorize training samples based on how easily they are learned by the model. By leveraging this technique, we systematically identify examples that may contribute to degraded model performance, such as mislabeled samples or outliers. Next, we integrate these identified bad seeds into the active learning framework to evaluate their impact on model improvement. Specifically, we examine whether prioritizing these challenging samples in the active learning process can enhance its efficiency in selecting informative instances for annotation. Unlike conventional active learning strategies that focus on uncertainty-based or diversity-based sampling~\cite{li2012sample,hu2021towards,hu2024active}, our method explicitly considers dataset quality by,

\begin{quote}
{\em Filtering out potentially harmful samples while selecting truly valuable ones.}
\end{quote}

By iteratively refining the training set in this manner, our method aims to accelerate model convergence with fewer annotated samples. The goal is to enhance model robustness by reducing the negative influence of noisy data while enabling active learning to quickly identify the most valuable training examples. Through this combined strategy, we can improve both dataset quality and model performance, ultimately making vulnerability detection more reliable with reduced annotation costs.

To demonstrate the effectiveness of our proposed method, we conduct a series of experiments. First, we generate dataset maps based on training dynamics to identify hard-to-learn samples. Second, we incorporate these hard-to-learn samples into active learning by refining the acquisition function, filtering out less informative data before updating the training set. Third, we evaluate the generalizability of our approach by extending the experiments to multiple models and datasets.

In summary, this paper makes the following contributions to the critical data challenge of vulnerability detection.

\begin{itemize}[wide=0pt]
    \item We assess whether hard-to-learn examples affect model performance.
    \item We introduce the use of dataset maps in vulnerability detection to identify bad seeds based on model learning dynamics.
    %We develop a new method to identify hard-to-learn examples by considering dataset maps and model learning dynamics. 
    \item We incorporate these bad seeds into active learning framework to evaluate their impact on improving training efficiency and performance. We conducted the evaluation in a model-invariant and dataset-invariant manner.
    \item We identify common features of outliers, providing insights for future improvements in dataset quality.
\end{itemize}



\section{Problem and Motivation}

A widely held belief in software engineering is that more data leads to better models. For example:

\begin{itemize}
  \item "..as long as it is large; the resulting prediction performance is likely to be boosted more by the size of the sample than it is hindered by any bias polarity that may exist"~\cite{rahman2013sample}. 
  \item "It is natural to think that a closer previous release has more similar characteristics and thus can help to train a more accurate defect prediction model. It is also natural to think that accumulating multiple releases can be beneficial because it represents the variability of a project"
  ~\cite{amasaki2020cross}.
  \item "Long-term JIT models should be trained using a cache of plenty of changes"~\cite{mcintosh2018fix}.
\end{itemize}

Similar to the defect prediction problem, vulnerability detection is also commonly formulated as a binary classification problem. Vulnerability detection aims to determine whether a given code $c$ is vulnerable or not, i.e., \[f(c) \to \mathcal{Y} \quad \text{while} \quad \mathcal{Y} = \{\textit{vulnerable}, \textit{nonVulnerable} \}\]

Recently, large language model (LLM)-based solutions have become the most effective solution for vulnerability detection~\cite{akuthota2023vulnerability,chakraborty2024revisiting,chakraborty2021deep,ding2024vulnerability,steenhoek2023empirical,lin2020software}.
As a method with a data-hungry nature, the effectiveness of LLM also generally follows \textit{more data leads to better performance}.
Considering the above, we are interested in answering the question,

\begin{quote}
\textit{Is the common sense of ``the more data the better'' hold in the existing vulnerability detection context?}
\end{quote}

To answer the question, we conduct a preliminary experiment on three commonly used vulnerability detection datasets, Big-Vul~\cite{fan2020ac}, Devign~\cite{zhou2019devign} and DiverseVul~\cite{chen2023diversevul}. For each dataset, we fine-tune a widely used LLM (i.e., CodeBERT~\cite{feng2020codebert}) based on 10 versions of datasets with gradually incremental sizes - starting from 10\% to 100\% with 10\% interval increase (i.e., 10\%, 20\%, ..., 100\%) — and evaluate the model's performance.
As shown in Figure \ref{fig:f1_accuracy}, we find that \textbf{the model trained on the full dataset does not achieve the highest performance}. Specifically, the model achieves the best performance when using only 70\% of the Big-Vul dataset, 80\% of the Devign dataset, and 40\% of the DiverseVul dataset, respectively. It indicates that some data instances may not be beneficial for boosting model performance. Instead, they may even harm the performance.

