Existing works provide some potential explanations for our results. For example,
Croft et al.~\cite{croft2023data} found that commonly used vulnerability datasets contain significant label inaccuracies and high duplication rates, which can mislead model training and give an inaccurate impression of performance in benchmarks. Wu et al.~\cite{wu2021data} further demonstrated that mislabeled instances in security bug report datasets mislead model learning, reducing predictive performance. Additionally, Hanif et al.~\cite{hanif2021rise} emphasized that while machine learning-based vulnerability detection has advanced, research on dataset quality remains limited, despite its direct influence on model robustness. 

However, none of the existing works further explore a solution based on the phenomenon. Motivated by the findings of our first attempt, we aim to answer the following question.

\begin{tcolorbox}
\textit{How to identify these harmful seeds to lead LLM to produce better performance even with fewer training data?}
\end{tcolorbox}


