\noindent\textbf{Experimental setting.}
To answer RQ1 and RQ2, we primarily focus on conducting experiments on the Big-Vul dataset (introduced in Section~\ref{sec:dataset}).
We randomly divide the dataset into two subsets with equal size, denoted as \textit{S1} and \textit{S2}.
Then, for each subset, we follow a standard ratio of 8:1:1 to split the subset into train, validation, and test sets. To address class imbalance in the training data, we include all available positive samples and randomly select an equal number of negative samples. This dataset setting follows the setup used in Yu's paper~\cite{yu2019improving} for evaluating active learning. 
%\bowen{can we find at least one ref to support the setting?}
% The positive samples are evenly divided between Subset1 and Subset2, with each subset paired with a corresponding number of negative samples to maintain balance.
Meanwhile, the validation and test sets retain the natural distribution of the dataset to reflect real-world conditions, where non-vulnerable code is significantly more prevalent than vulnerable code.
We process \textit{S1} and \textit{S2} in the same manner. Then, we use \textit{S1} for answering RQ1 and use the identified bad seeds to perform active learning on \textit{S2} for answering RQ2.

We train the CodeBERT model on \textit{S1} for 10 epochs. During training, we record the model's predicted logits for each sample at every epoch. Since the first two epochs alone often do not provide stable and sufficient information to assess how a sample's logits evolve over time, we begin tracking two key statistics from the 3rd to the 10th epoch: the number of times a sample has been correctly classified up to that point and instances where a previously correct prediction is forgotten.
% With only two recorded values, it is difficult to determine whether a sample exhibits consistent learning difficulties or fluctuations in prediction confidence.
% The first two epochs are excluded from this analysis since they lack sufficient preceding data to capture meaningful trends. \bowen{can you justify the magic number 3 here? any refernce can support why is 3? why not 2 or 4, or other numbers?}
Using these dynamics, we compute a difficulty metric based on confidence and variability, where confidence measures the certainty in model's predictions, and variability captures the frequency of prediction changes across epochs (detailed in Section~\ref{sec:dm}). 
After calculating confidence and variability for all samples, we define bad seeds as those whose confidence and variability values fall below the threshold values of 0.3 and 0.4, respectively. The threshold values of 0.3 for confidence and 0.4 for variability are selected based on empirical observations of dataset maps across multiple experimental runs. These parameters are determined by enumerating configurations and assessing their impact on the validation data. To maintain consistency and control variables in subsequent experiments, we use the same threshold settings across all the trials. Bad seeds are identified separately at the 3rd and 10th epochs, resulting in different sets at different training stages.

To determine which set of bad seeds has the most negative impact on training, we
systematically remove them from the full \textit{S1} dataset and retrain the model on the remaining data. To ensure that the observed improvements are driven by the dataset maps' selection rather than the removal process itself, we conduct an additional experiment where, at each epoch, we randomly remove the same number of samples five times and retrained the model under the same parameter settings.

