\section{Discussion}

\subsection{Towards better-and-also-stable active learn in vulnerability detection}

In Hu et al.'s paper~\cite{hu2024active}, their original active learning study applied DeepGini and K-Means only on Devign, a balanced dataset, and observed that both acquisition functions consistently outperformed random selection. However, this dataset carries a certain limitation, i.e., does not reflect the real-world distribution of vulnerabilities, where non-vulnerable samples significantly outnumber vulnerable ones. To better evaluate active learning in a more realistic scenario, we expand the experiments on BigVul and DiverseVul, the imbalanced datasets.
We find that standard active learning strategies do not always outperform random selection in these more practical scenarios. This result suggests that conventional acquisition functions do not always prioritize samples well.
% , particularly in datasets with inherent class imbalance.
% This finding suggests that existing acquisition functions do not always prioritize truly informative samples when expanding the seed set, highlighting a key limitation of current methods.

Differently, the active learning enhanced by our proposed approach can produce not only better but also consistent performance.
Additionally, we observe that as active learning progresses through multiple iterations, both standard DeepGini and K-Means exhibit performance fluctuations. This instability arises due to the nature of their selection mechanisms—DeepGini's reliance on uncertainty-based sampling and K-Means’ clustering-based approach introducing variability in data acquisition. We quantify this fluctuation by computing the standard deviation of performance across all experiments in previous research questions, covering three datasets and three models. The results indicate that standard DeepGini and K-Means have significantly higher variance (0.0603 and 0.0685, respectively) compared to our proposed method (0.0442 for DeepGini and 0.0328 for K-Means), demonstrating improved stability. Specifically, our approach reduces variance by 26.7\% for DeepGini and 52.1\% for K-Means, ensuring more consistent training performance across iterations.

Our method, despite some fluctuations, demonstrates reduced variance compared to the standard versions, indicating that filtering bad seed examples leads to a more stable training process. Furthermore, our proposed approach consistently outperforms both standard methods and random selection in the later stages of active learning, reinforcing the benefit of incorporating dataset maps into acquisition functions. These results suggest that bad seeds filtering not only enhances active learning's effectiveness but also mitigates the risk of selecting bad seeds, ultimately leading to improved model performance in vulnerability detection.

\subsection{What Makes a Common Bad Seed?}
%Based on our results, we observe that different models identify varying sets of bad seeds within the same dataset, suggesting that bad seeds are model-dependent. To better understand this phenomenon, we intend to analyze the common characteristics among these samples, aiming to uncover patterns that contribute to their learning difficulty across different architectures.
Based on our results of RQ2 and RQ3 on Big-Vul \textit{S1}, we observe that CodeBERT, GraphCodeBERT, and CodeT5 identify 207, 197, and 45 samples, respectively, as bad seeds. For the other two datasets, Devign and DiverseVul, CodeBERT identifies 148 and 397 bad seeds. To better understand the nature of these samples, we analyze their distribution and compare them with their respective \textit{S1} datasets. Through this analysis, we identify following findings on bad seeds in vulnerability detection datasets.

\begin{itemize}[wide=0pt]
    \item \textbf{Bad seeds are more commonly identified in non-vulnerable samples even if the training data is balanced}.
    To address the challenge of highly imbalanced problem nature, we follow prior work~\cite{yu2019improving} to construct a balanced dataset for model training. In other words, the training data of \textit{S1} is constructed as  with an equal number of vulnerable and non-vulnerable samples, i.e., 50\% for each category. However, we find that bad seeds are more commonly exist in non-vulnerable samples. More specifically, among the bad seeds identified by CodeBERT, GraphCodeBERT, and CodeT5 in Big-Vul, non-vulnerable samples account for 79.2\%, 76.1\%, and 80.0\%, respectively. To further validate this trend, we examine the proportion of non-vulnerable samples among the bad seeds identified in Devign and DiverseVul, which are 61.5\% and 84.4\%, respectively. This contradicts the common expectation that models struggle more with learning vulnerable samples.
    Moreover, current vulnerability detection datasets ~\cite{fan2020ac,chakraborty2021deep,nikitopoulos2021crossvul,bhandari2021cvefixes}, are constructed by extracting vulnerable functions from security-fixing commits and non-vulnerable functions from code snapshots taken when no known vulnerabilities were reported. However, this approach may mislabel undetected vulnerabilities as non-vulnerable, leading to misleading training and performance evaluation, which could explain the higher prevalence of bad seeds among non-vulnerable samples.
    %\bowen{can we talk a bit about how the existing datasets are constructed? (give at least one concrete example) what are the limitations related to our finding?} 
    Instead, our results suggest that \textbf{more attention is needed on examining the label quality of non-vulnerable samples in vulnerability datasets}, making them harder for models to learn effectively. Based on our finding, we highlight the importance of ensuring quality of the samples on both sides and advocate to develop more solutions to examine non-vulnerable labels when constructing vulnerability detection datasets.

    \item \textbf{Surprisingly, bad seeds exist in vulnerable code tend be shorter, can be patched with smaller code change, and also less complex than the average.}

    Intuitively, the more complex the code, the harder to learn. Therefore, we hypothesize that bad seeds are often appear to be complex than average.
    To verify this, we use three indicators to measure the complexity of vulnerability data, (1) code token length, (2) patch size, and (3) Cyclomatic Complexity.

    \begin{enumerate}[wide=0pt]
        \item \textbf{Code length} is often considered as an indicator of code complexity~\cite{munoz2020empirical,klima2022selected,peitek2021program}
        %\bowen{add refs}. 
        Considering the training paradigm of LLM, especially tokenization, we particularly focus on token length analysis.
        We aggregate all bad seeds identified by CodeBERT, GraphCodeBERT, and CodeT5 in Big-Vul and extract 81 vulnerable functions to compare with the complete set of vulnerable functions in \textit{S1}. We find that the average token length of vulnerable functions in \textit{S1} is 531, whereas, surprisingly, in the bad seeds, the average token length is only 104 which is significantly lower. We also perform the same analysis on DiverseVul and Devign. We find that, for DiverseVul, the average token length of vulnerable functions is 766, whereas in bad seeds, it also dramatically lower (89\% relatively) to only 82.7. However, Devign is the exception, we find that the difference is minimal, with the original and bad seed functions having token lengths of 504 and 478, respectively.    

        \item \textbf{Patch size} can also be considered as another indicator of complexity in vulnerability context. We hypothesize that, bad seeds tend to be the vulnerable code which require bigger changes to be patched. Fortunately, Big-Vul provides patch information which enables us to compare function modifications before and after patching. Note that, Devign and DiverseVul do not provide patch information. We calculate that, the average patch size (sum of added and deleted lines) in the original \textit{S1} is 9.6, while the patch size of bad seeds is only 5.65 (41\% smaller).

        \item \textbf{Cyclomatic Complexity} has also been used analyze the complexity of the code samples. Specifically, we use the Lizard~\cite{lizard} tool to compute the complexity of C/C++ functions. In Big-Vul, the average complexity of vulnerable functions in bad seeds is 3.58, compared to 14.8 in the full \textit{S1}. A similar trend is observed in DiverseVul, where the original vulnerable functions have an average complexity of 22.92, whereas the bad seeds have a much lower complexity of 2.43. In contrast, again, Devign does not exhibit this trend, with complexity values for the original and bad seed functions being 11.95 and 11.76, respectively. This suggests that in more realistic datasets, vulnerable functions with lower complexity are more likely to be classified as bad seeds, possibly because they lack structural patterns that models rely on for learning vulnerability detection.
    \end{enumerate}
    %\item  A significant number of these functions involve intricate pointer operations and dynamic memory management. Functions such as \texttt{clone\_mnt()}, \texttt{fixup\_bpf\_calls()}, and \texttt{g2m\_init\_buffers()} frequently allocate, deallocate, and manipulate memory buffers. For instance, in \texttt{g2m\_init\_buffers()}, memory is dynamically allocated using \texttt{av\_mallocz()}, but there is no explicit deallocation in case of failure, potentially leading to memory leaks. Improper pointer checks or dereferencing uninitialized pointers can result in null pointer dereference or use-after-free vulnerabilities. Models often struggle to detect these issues due to the implicit dependencies between memory allocations and their usage, requiring deeper data flow analysis beyond syntactic pattern matching. 
    %\item Another challenging feature is the parsing and processing of binary data, which appears in functions handling structured formats such as MNG, PDF, MPEG4, and ELF files. Functions like \texttt{ReadOneMNGImage()}, \texttt{ReadPDFImage()}, and \texttt{dophn\_core()} manipulate raw byte streams and perform operations such as direct byte comparisons or manual offset calculations, increasing the risk of out-of-bounds access and integer overflows, particularly when processing malformed or malicious inputs. Traditional vulnerability detection models find these cases difficult because they require semantic understanding of file formats, the ability to infer constraints on memory access through byte-level operations, and the detection of hidden implicit control flows based on parsed data values. 
    %\item Furthermore, many functions contain deeply nested conditional branches and complex error handling mechanisms, as seen in\\ \texttt{print\_lcp\_config\_options()}. This function validates input conditions in multiple stages, but incorrect assumptions in early checks can lead to unexpected behaviors later. Models struggle with such vulnerabilities due to the necessity for path-sensitive analysis across multiple execution branches, implicit state transitions that are not easily inferred from local context, and nested dependencies on earlier conditions, making them difficult to generalize across different functions. 
\end{itemize}

In summary, our find that, our analysis on Big-Vul and DiverseVul datasets does not follow the common intuition, i.e., \textit{the more complex, the harder to learn}. Instead, it indicates the opposite conclusion, i.e., bad seeds in the vulnerable category seems more tend to be ``simpler'' than the average.
Our observation reveal a specific focus that vulnerability detection studies often overlooked.
Moreover, we also observe that, the Devign dataset is often the exception.
The potential reason could be that, in more realistic (i.e., highly imbalanced) datasets like Big-Vul and DiverseVul, functions with smaller token sizes and smaller patches are more likely to be classified as bad seeds.

%Given that our study mainly focuses on C/C++ vulnerability datasets, we advocate further investigation to investigate whether our findings are generalizable to other programming languages.



% Since our study focuses on only three models on the Big-Vul dataset, the identified bad seeds are specific to Big-Vul. For other datasets, additional analysis is required to determine common bad seeds across different datasets and models.
% We advocate more future efforts to expand this investigation to more datasets and architectures to generalize these findings and further improve the robustness of vulnerability detection models.



\subsection{Threats to Validity}
%The effectiveness of dataset maps in identifying bad seeds samples depends on the training dynamics at different epochs. As training progresses, the model's ability to distinguish between informative and misleading samples improves, influencing the composition of dataset maps. However, the optimal epoch for generating dataset maps is not fixed and can vary across models and datasets. The training dynamics at earlier epochs may differ significantly from those at later stages, impacting the selection of bad seed samples. To systematically investigate this, our experiment spans epochs 3 to 10, ensuring that we capture a broad range of training dynamics and determine the most effective dataset maps for sample removal.

%Additionally, the threshold values of 0.3 for confidence and 0.4 for variability were selected based on empirical observations of dataset maps across multiple experimental runs. These parameters were determined by testing various configurations and analyzing their impact on identifying bad seed examples. To maintain consistency and control variables in subsequent experiments, we fixed these values across all trials. However, the selection of these thresholds remains an open question, and further exploration is necessary to optimize dataset maps for different models and datasets. Future work should examine whether alternative parameter choices could further enhance the identification of bad seed samples and improve model performance.

One threat to internal validity is that, in RQ2, our proposed method modifies the standard active learning pipeline by selecting 125 candidates per iteration instead of 100, then removing the top 20\% most similar to previously identified bad seeds. The 20\% filtering ratio is determined as a reasonable balance between preserving valuable data and removing misleading samples. However, we have not exhaustively tested with alternative ratios. While our experimental setup is consistent, further research is necessary to investigate how different filtering thresholds affect active learning performance. Another threat lies in the parameter setting for identifying bad seeds in RQ1. We enumerate different values of confidence and variability thresholds and based on the empirical results on the validation data, we set confidence to 0.3 and variability to 0.4. To mitigate this threat, we plan to develop an effective and also automated way to identify the optimal values of these thresholds in our future work.

Regarding external validity, one is about the generalizability of our results. In our RQ3, we have specifically investigated this threat by experimenting with more models and datasets. This minimizes concerns about dataset and model dependency.
Another threat is that we only evaluate C/C++ vulnerability datasets, and its generalizability to other languages remains uncertain. Future work will extend the study to other languages and incorporate additional metrics for a more comprehensive evaluation.

\section{Related Works}

\subsection{LLM for Vulnerability Detection}
%For vulnerability detection, One of the key challenges in vulnerability detection using LLMs is the quality of training datasets, which has recently drawn significant attention. Several studies have highlighted that existing vulnerability detection datasets often contain noisy, mislabeled, or imbalanced samples, which negatively impact model performance. For instance, Ding et al.~\cite{ding2024vulnerability} found that existing datasets suffer from low label accuracy and high duplicate rates, which lead to unreliable model performance in real scenarios. Previous studies yield mixed results: Khare et al.~\cite{khare2023understanding} showed that LLMs perform well on synthetic datasets but not promising on real-world datasets.In our work, we apply dataset maps to vulnerability detection to systematically analyze training data and improve model performance. We leverage dataset maps to identify bad seeds—samples that negatively impact training—and evaluate their influence on model learning. By removing these samples and comparing performance changes, we assess their role in model generalization. 
Recent studies have explored the use of Large Language Models (LLMs) for vulnerability detection, evaluating their performance and limitations~\cite{akuthota2023vulnerability,chakraborty2024revisiting,chen2023diversevul,zhou2024large,shestov2025finetuning}. Chakraborty et al.~\cite{chakraborty2024revisiting} analyzed CodeBERT and GraphCodeBERT on vulnerability datasets, finding that while LLMs capture semantic patterns, they are sensitive to mislabeling and data imbalance, impacting detection reliability. Ding et al.~\cite{ding2024vulnerability} evaluated CodeBERT and CodeT5 across multiple datasets, identifying issues with dataset duplication and label inconsistencies, which affect model generalization. They highlighted that LLMs outperform traditional ML models but require cleaner datasets. Zhou et al.~\cite{zhou2024large} examined GPT-based models for large-scale software security, showing that they generalize well for syntactic vulnerabilities but struggle with pointer manipulations and complex control flows. They suggested combining LLMs with program analysis techniques. Chen et al.~\cite{chen2023diversevul} showed that increasing the volume of training data may not further improve the performance of deep learning models for vulnerability
detection.

Although LLMs can be pretrained on large-scale data in an unsupervised manner, poor-quality or mislabeled datasets hinder their generalization, robustness, and reliability. Addressing these challenges requires better data curation and model refinement.

%\bowen{at the end, we should say something about ``although LLM can be pretrained by learning from massive data in unsupervised manner, what limits the power of the LLM is the lack of the good data''.}

\subsection{Active Learning for Vulnerability Detection}
Active learning has been applied in various software engineering tasks to reduce labeling costs and improve model efficiency. Moskovitch et al.~\cite{moskovitch2008malicious} proposed an early framework utilizing active learning for detecting malicious code. Yu et al.~\cite{yu2019improving} introduced HARMLESS, an active learning-based vulnerability inspection tool that incrementally refines a support vector machine model to guide security experts toward high-risk code regions, effectively reducing the effort needed for vulnerability detection. Lu et al.~\cite{lu2014defect} applied active learning to defect prediction across software versions, combining uncertainty sampling with dimensionality reduction to improve defect detection performance. More recently, Hu et al.~\cite{hu2024active} introduced the first active learning benchmark for code-related tasks, including vulnerability detection, evaluating over ten acquisition functions on pre-trained code models. 

While these works have demonstrated the potential of active learning in vulnerability detection, they primarily focus on acquisition functions without considering the inherent quality of training data. Given that vulnerability datasets often contain mislabeled or low-quality samples, existing active learning approaches may fail to consistently select valuable instances for model training. In our work, we address this limitation by integrating dataset maps into active learning.
% Dataset map has been widely applied in computer vision~\cite{he2024large,karamcheti2021mind,kye2023tidal} and NLP tasks~\cite{kiela2021dynabench,rodriguez2021evaluation,zhang2022survey}.
