% It's already applied in computer vision\cite{he2024large}\cite{karamcheti2021mind}\cite{kye2023tidal} and NLP task~\cite{kiela2021dynabench}\cite{rodriguez2021evaluation}\cite{zhang2022survey}.
\section{Conclusions}
In this work, we investigate one of the key challenges of vulnerability detection, i.e., data quality. To address the challenge, we propose an approach that integrates dataset maps with active learning to improve model performance. The key idea behind our proposed method is to leverage the signals from model training dynamics to identify bad seeds —that negatively affect model training. Our results demonstrate that removing these bad seeds can lead to significant performance gains.
% However, the effectiveness of dataset maps varies across different training stages, highlighting the importance of selecting the most informative training epochs for identifying bad seeds.
Building on this, we explore the potential of optimizing active learning for vulnerability detection by identifying bad seeds. While existing active learning methods primarily focus on refining acquisition functions, our findings reveal that these approaches may not always outperform random selection.
% By incorporating our method into the acquisition function, we filtered out bad seeds samples, ensuring that selected instances contributed effectively to model training.
Our results demonstrate that, our proposed method can consistently outperformed both standard active learning and random selection, leading to more stable improvements in model performance on multiple models and datasets.
% The results indicated that different models identified distinct sets of bad seeds, reinforcing the model-dependent nature of bad seeds samples.
% Moreover, our findings across different datasets confirmed that dataset maps consistently enhanced active learning effectiveness, although the extent of improvement varied based on dataset characteristics. These results underscore the necessity of adapting dataset maps to specific models and datasets to maximize their benefits.

%Overall, our study highlights the limitations of traditional active learning in vulnerability detection and demonstrates the effectiveness of dataset maps in refining both dataset quality and acquisition function performance. Future work could explore adaptive strategies for selecting optimal training epochs for dataset maps generation and further investigate the generalization of this approach across additional security-related tasks.

