\begin{proof}[Proof of Lemma~\ref{lemma: Mercer}]
Assumption~\ref{assumption: GP kernel} ensures that the kernel \( h \) is continuous, symmetric, and positive semi-definite on the compact domain \( \mathcal{X} = \mathcal{H}_k \times \mathcal{A}_k \), and that it satisfies the integrability condition
\[
\int_{\mathcal{X} \times \mathcal{X}} h(x, x') \, \rho(x) \rho(x') \, dx \, dx' < \infty.
\]
These conditions match those required for Mercer's Theorem (see Section 12.3 of \citet{Wainwright2019}). Therefore, the existence of the spectral decomposition described in Equations~\eqref{eq: lemma Mercer eq 1} and~\eqref{eq: lemma Mercer eq 2} follows directly from an application of the theorem.
