\section{Related Work}
We discuss two lines of related work: MLLMs in DocQA tasks and methods used in DocQA tasks.

\paragraph{MLLMs in DocQA Tasks.} 
Recent Document Visual Question Answering (DocVQA) models focus on expanding context window sizes, improving fine-grained visual comprehension, and enhancing layout analysis (\cite{hu2024mplug, liu2024textmonkey, tworkowski2023focused}). These systems often boost performance through architectural enhancements (e.g., added modules) or multi-stage training pipelines with diverse data inputs (\cite{park2024hierarchical, tito2023hierarchical, chen2023longlora}). While general multimodal models like Gemini (\cite{team2024gemini}) and Qwen-VL (\cite{Qwen-VL}) show improved visual processing and context handling, they remain constrained by input length limitations and multi-page processing capabilities. Their reliance on image-derived representations also hinders precise localization of detailed elements, while layout extraction from visual data risks diverting the model's focus, reducing answer accuracy (\cite{han2025mdocagent}).
% Models specifically designed for Document Visual Question Answering (DocVQA) have been continuously proposed (\cite{hu2024mplug,liu2024textmonkey,tworkowski2023focused}). The main approaches of these models generally focus on increasing the content window size, improving the understanding of fine-grained image information, or enhancing layout comprehension (\cite{luo2024layoutllm}). These models improve reading comprehension abilities for short or long documents by adding modules to the base model or designing multi-stage, multi-type data as training input (\cite{park2024hierarchical,tito2023hierarchical,chen2023longlora}). With the development of general-purpose multimodal large models, issues related to content windows and image understanding have gradually improved, allowing users to directly call pre-trained models to answer document-related questions (\cite{team2024gemini,Qwen-VL,marafioti2025smolvlm,peng2023yarn,bai2024longalign}). However, multimodal large models are still limited by input window size and cannot process multiple pages at once. Additionally, these models convert various types of information in images into visual representations, which makes it difficult to precisely locate fine-grained elements. Meanwhile, relevant layout information can only be obtained from the images, which distracts the model’s attention and results in lower accuracy when directly using multimodal large models to answer document-related questions (\cite{han2025mdocagent}).




\paragraph{Methods in DocQA Tasks.}
Retrieval augmentation has become pivotal for adapting general multimodal models to document question answering, addressing input constraints and information overload by retrieving fine-grained evidence through multimodal or unimodal queries (\cite{lewis2020retrieval, gao2023retrieval, chen2024mllm, cho2024m3docrag}). While current methods enhance accuracy through optimized retrieval strategies (\cite{deng2024longdocurl, lu2024text}), input formatting remains challenging: image-only inputs hinder textual comprehension due to incomplete OCR integration, whereas text-only inputs (via OCR) neglect visual elements critical for image-dependent questions. Multimodal inputs combining text and images risk overwhelming models with redundant or conflicting data, leading to attention dispersion. To maximize performance, refining input strategies—such as modality prioritization, structured evidence fusion, or dynamic filtering—is essential for balancing information richness and focused reasoning in retrieved evidence.
% To better utilize general-purpose multimodal large models for answering document-related questions, retrieval augmentation has become an essential part of current document reading methods (\cite{lewis2020retrieval,gao2023retrieval}). Through retrieval augmentation, using unimodal or multimodal information as queries, more fine-grained evidence can be located, and the relevant evidence along with the question is then passed into the general large model (\cite{chen2024mllm,cho2024m3docrag}). This helps alleviate issues such as input window size limitations and excessive information interference. By designing different retrieval augmentation methods, evidence can be located more accurately, thus enhancing the large model’s ability to answer questions (\cite{deng2024longdocurl,cho2024m3docrag,lu2024text}). After retrieval augmentation, current methods either input unimodal information, such as evidence images alone or text extracted from evidence via OCR, or a combination of both. However, when only images are provided, multimodal large models struggle to fully utilize the textual information in the images, leading to loss of relevant information during the conversion of text into visual representations. If only OCR-extracted text is used as input, multimodal large models cannot answer questions that require viewing images in the document. When both unimodal and multimodal information are used as input, excessive information may interfere with the model’s judgment and distract its attention. Therefore, once retrieval augmentation locates the relevant evidence pages, exploring how to enhance the large model’s ability to answer document-related questions and leverage multiple types of elements by modifying the input format or method is a worthwhile issue.

