{In this section, we analyze the autocorrelation properties and ambiguity functions of Gold and ZC sequences, which are widely used for reference signals in legacy GNSS systems and TNs, and compare them with those of Bj\"orck sequences. This analysis establishes the potential of Bj\"orck sequences for accurate delay-Doppler estimation and highlights their suitability as a candidate for reference signals in LEO-based PNT systems. To this end, we first discuss the generation of these sequences and then conduct a comparative analysis of their correlation properties and ambiguity functions.}

{\em Generation of Gold Sequences.} Reference signals using Gold sequences~\cite{3gpp::38211} are generated as follows.
\begin{align}
    & \mathbf{r}(n) = \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}} (1-2\mathbf{c}(2n)) + j\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}(1-2\mathbf{c}(2n+1)),\notag\\
    & \mathbf{c}(i) = (\mathbf{x}_1(i+N_c)+\mathbf{x}_2(i+N_c))\mod 2,\notag\\
    & \mathbf{x}_1(i+31) = (\mathbf{x}_1(i+3)+\mathbf{x}_1(i))\mod 2,\notag\\
    & \mathbf{x}_2(i+31) = \notag\\&\quad(\mathbf{x}_2(i+3)+\mathbf{x}_2(i+2)+\mathbf{x}_2(i+1)+\mathbf{x}_2(i))\mod 2,
    \label{eq:gold_seq_gen}
\end{align}
where \(\mathbf{c}(\cdot)\) is a Gold sequence, \(N_c=1600\), \(\mathbf{x}_1(0) = 1, \mathbf{x}_1(i) = 0, i=1,2,\cdots,30\), and \(\mathbf{x}_2(\cdot)\) is defined by \(c_{\rm init} = \sum_{i=0}^{30}\mathbf{x}_2(i)\cdot2^i\). The initial seed \(c_{\rm init}\) depends on the application, e.g., for PRS, \(c_{\rm init}\) is derived from the PRS sequence ID and the slot number within the radio frame. 

{\em Generation of ZC Sequences.} Reference signals using ZC sequences~\cite{andrews2023primerzadoffchusequences,3gpp::38211,10632801dureppagari_uav2} are generated as follows.
\begin{align}
    & \bar{\mathbf{r}}_{u,v}(n) = \mathbf{x}_q(n\mod N_{\rm ZC}),\quad n = 0,...,M_{\rm ZC}-1,\notag\\
    & \mathbf{x}_q(m) = e^{-j\frac{\pi qm(m+1)}{N_{\rm ZC}}},
    \label{eq:gold_seq_gen}
\end{align}
where \(M_{\rm ZC}\) is the length of the sequence, \(N_{\rm ZC}\) is the largest prime number less than \(M_{\rm ZC}\). The root index \(q\) is defined as \(q=\lfloor\bar{q}+\frac{1}{2}\rfloor+v\cdot(-1)^{\lfloor2\bar{q}\rfloor}\), where \(\bar{q}=N_{\rm ZC}\cdot\frac{(u+1)}{31}\). Here, \(u\in\{0,...,29\}\) is the group number, and \(v\) is the base sequence number within the group. Subsequently, \( \mathbf{x}_{prs}(\cdot) \) in~\eqref{eq:rx_sgl_model} is generated by mapping \(\mathbf{r}(\cdot)\) or \(\bar{\mathbf{r}}_{u,v}(\cdot)\) onto the orthogonal frequency division multiplexing (OFDM) resource grid, according to the time and frequency domain resource allocation~\cite{3gpp::38211}.

