% However, a key question arises: Why not use CAZAC sequences, such as ZC sequences~\cite{1054840}, which are already employed for other reference signals like demodulation reference signals (DMRS) and SRS~\cite{3gpp::38211,10632801}? CAZAC sequences are particularly appealing because of their constant amplitude property, which helps reduce the peak-to-average power ratio (PAPR)~\cite{khan2020low}, and zero cyclic autocorrelation property~\cite{andrews2023primerzadoffchusequences}, which helps reduce the sidelobe level. This reduction in PAPR is especially beneficial for power-limited use cases, e.g., in UL, where UE power efficiency is crucial. To address this question, we assess the autocorrelation and ambiguity functions of both Gold and ZC sequences. Fig.~\ref{fig:autocorr_pn} and Fig.~\ref{fig:autocorr_zc} present the autocorrelation properties, while Fig.~\ref{fig:ambiguity_pn} and Fig.~\ref{fig:ambiguity_zc} depict their ambiguity functions, generated using the~\eqref{eq:ambiguity_fn}. For this assessment, we consider \(1\) MHz bandwidth and \(15\) kHz SCS, leading to sequences of length 59. The results indicate that while ZC sequences exhibit superior correlation properties, Gold sequences outperform them in terms of their ambiguity function, which is crucial for accurate delay and Doppler estimation. This makes ZC sequences less suitable for high Doppler scenarios, e.g., LEO-based systems (Doppler shifts typically on the order of 30-40 kHz). Such scenarios require  Doppler shifts to be accurately estimated and corrected, even if Doppler-based positioning methods are not explicitly employed, as they can introduce significant range errors~\cite{9144242}. Given these findings, we pose the question: Are there other CAZAC sequences that could serve as viable alternatives to Gold sequences for PRS generation? In response, we propose using Bj\"orck sequences, which are also CAZAC sequences~\cite{BjrckAmbiguity4775877}, for PRS generation. In this context, we will discuss the generation of Bj\"orck sequences, their correlation properties, and their ambiguity function, highlighting how they can serve as a viable alternative for PRS generation. 

{\em Generation of Bj\"orck Sequences.} Like ZC sequences, Bj\"orck sequences are only defined for prime lengths~\cite{Bjorck1990,BjrckAmbiguity4775877}. Given a prime number \(P\), Bj\"orck sequences are expressed as
\begin{align}
    \mathbf{b}_P(m) = e^{j\boldsymbol{\theta}_P(m)}, \quad m = 0, 1, \cdots, P-1,
\end{align}
where \(\boldsymbol{\theta}_P(m)\) is defined for two cases. For \(P \equiv 1 \pmod{4}\), we have
\begin{align}
    \boldsymbol{\theta}_P(m) = \genfrac{(}{)}{}{}{m}{P} \arccos\left(\frac{1}{1+\sqrt{P}}\right),
    \label{eq:bjrck_case1}
\end{align}
and for \(P \equiv 3 \pmod{4}\), we have
\begin{align}
    \boldsymbol{\theta}_P(m) = 
    \begin{cases} 
        \arccos\left(\frac{1-P}{1+P}\right), & \text{if } \genfrac{(}{)}{}{}{m}{P} = -1, \\
        0, & \text{otherwise}.
    \end{cases}
    \label{eq:bjrck_case2}
\end{align}

Here, \(\genfrac{(}{)}{}{}{m}{P}\) is a Legendre symbol defined as
\begin{align}
    \genfrac{(}{)}{}{}{m}{P} =
    \begin{cases} 
        0, & \text{if } m \equiv 0 \pmod{P}, \\
        1, & \text{if } m \text{ is a quadratic residue modulo }P, \\
        -1, & \text{if } m \text{ is a quadratic nonresidue modulo }P.
    \end{cases}
\end{align}
