Orthogonal Bj\"orck sequences of length \(P\) can be constructed using circulant matrices. Denoting the resultant circulant matrix by \(\mathbf{B}_P\), orthogonal sequences are generated from the base sequence \(\mathbf{b}_P(\cdot)\) as
\begin{align}
    \mathbf{B}_P = \begin{bmatrix}
        \mathbf{b}_P(0) & \mathbf{b}_P(P-1) & \cdots & \mathbf{b}_P(1)\\
        \mathbf{b}_P(1) & \mathbf{b}_P(0) & \cdots & \mathbf{b}_P(2)\\
        \vdots & \vdots & \ddots & \vdots\\
        \mathbf{b}_P(P-1) & \mathbf{b}_P(P-2) & \cdots & \mathbf{b}_P(0)
    \end{bmatrix}.
    \label{eq:circulant_bjorck_B^P_C}
\end{align}

{The matrix \( \mathbf{B}_P \) is scaled unitary, i.e., its columns are mutually orthogonal and \(\frac{1}{P}\mathbf{B}_P\mathbf{B}_P^H=\mathbf{I}_P\), where \(\mathbf{I}_P\) is an identity matrix of size \(P\times P\), and each column serves as a distinct sequence. Notably, these sequences retain their CAZAC properties even after undergoing a Fourier transform, which is particularly advantageous in OFDM-based systems.} This is achieved due to the Fourier duality property of Bj\"orck sequences, as stated in the following theorem without proof.
\begin{theorem}[Fourier Duality of Bj\"orck Sequences~\cite{fourierdualsbjorck}]
    The Fourier dual of a Bj\"orck sequence depends on its prime-length classification:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item For \( P \equiv 1 \mod 4 \), the Bj\"orck sequence is self-dual, i.e., its {P-point} Discrete Fourier Transform (DFT) also forms a CAZAC sequence with identical properties.
        \item For \( P \equiv 3 \mod 4 \), the Fourier dual is not identical to the original sequence but is instead a scaled version of another CAZAC sequence, obtained by modifying the first element of the sequence while preserving its ideal periodic autocorrelation properties.
    \end{itemize}
\end{theorem}

{Note that in practical OFDM-based systems, an \(N\)-point inverse Fast Fourier Transform (IFFT) is applied to a sequence of length \(P\), where \(N\) is typically a power of 2 and \(N > P\). As a result, the transmitted signal will be an oversampled version of the original sequence.} An approach similar to~\eqref{eq:circulant_bjorck_B^P_C} is employed in 5G NR for preamble generation as part of PRACH transmission~\cite{3gpp::38211}. In this context, different preamble sequences are generated by applying cyclic shifts to the root ZC sequence in the time domain. These cyclic shifts are carefully determined to ensure that the minimum distance between any two sequences exceeds the maximum delay uncertainty~\cite{8517052}.

Next, we assess the autocorrelation and ambiguity function of Gold, ZC, and Bj\"orck sequences. Fig.~\ref{fig:autocorr_xcorr} present the autocorrelation properties, while Fig.~\ref{fig:ambiguity_fn} depict their ambiguity functions, generated using~\eqref{eq:ambiguity_fn}. For this assessment, we consider \(1\) MHz bandwidth and \(15\) kHz SCS, leading to sequences of length \(60\). 
{The extension of Bj\"orck sequences to non-prime lengths will be discussed in Section~\ref{sec:cross_corr_bjorck}. The results indicate that while ZC sequences exhibit superior autocorrelation properties compared to Gold sequences, their ambiguity function contains multiple false peaks, which can impair accurate delay estimation in high Doppler environments.}
