The generation of even-length sequences is particularly relevant for OFDM systems~\cite{3gpp::38211}, where \( N \) is typically an even number and a multiple of \(12\). {Since both \( \mathbf{B}_{Q_1} \) and \( \mathbf{B}_{Q_2} \) are circulant matrices derived from Bj\"orck sequences, they exhibit perfect orthogonality within each block.} However, the process of repeatedly appending shorter-length sequences (i.e., $\mathbf{B}_{Q_2}$) to longer-length sequences (i.e., $\mathbf{B}_{Q_1}$) introduces overlap regions,  i.e., certain columns of \( \mathbf{B}_N \) share the same sequence from \( \mathbf{B}_{Q_2} \), resulting in nonzero cross-correlation between those columns. We now derive the upper bound for the mean cross-correlation for this construction. % Despite this, we show that the average cross-correlation across all column pairs remains strictly bounded below \(\frac{1}{N}\).

\begin{lemma}[Mean Cross-Correlation Bound for Even $N$]\label{lem:evenXcorr}
Let $N = Q_1 + Q_2$, where $Q_1$ and $Q_2$ are prime numbers, and let \( Q_1 = \max(Q_1, Q_2) \). The resultant Bj\"orck sequence circulant matrix $\mathbf{B}_N$ is constructed by repeatedly appending columns of $\mathbf{B}_{Q_2}$ to $\mathbf{B}_{Q_1}$. Let $\mu_{C,N}^B$ denote the mean cross-correlation considering the overall pair of columns in $\mathbf{B}_N$. Then, the mean cross-correlation satisfies the bound:
\begin{align}
    \mu_{C,N}^B \;<\; \frac{1}{N}.
\end{align}
\end{lemma}
\begin{IEEEproof}  
%In the construction of \( \mathbf{B}_N \), each column is formed by appending a sequence from \( \mathbf{B}_{Q_2} \) to a sequence from \( \mathbf{B}_{Q_1} \). 
To analyze the mean cross-correlation across \( \mathbf{B}_N \), we consider pairwise inner products between its \( Q_1 \) column vectors:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Any two columns that use different sequences from \( \mathbf{B}_{Q_2} \) remain orthogonal, contributing zero cross-correlation.
    \item Columns that reuse the same sequence from \( \mathbf{B}_{Q_2} \) exhibit nonzero cross-correlation only in the appended portion. Since the overlapping length is \( Q_2 \) out of the total length \( N \), each such pair contributes a cross-correlation of \( \frac{Q_2}{N} \).
\end{itemize}

{Each unique sequence from \( \mathbf{B}_{Q_2} \) is repeated at most \( \lfloor Q_1 / Q_2 \rfloor \) times. Therefore, each column in \( \mathbf{B}_N \) has at most \( \lfloor Q_1 / Q_2 \rfloor \) non-orthogonal counterparts (i.e., columns with identical \( \mathbf{B}_{Q_2} \) segments), each contributing cross-correlation \( \frac{Q_2}{N} \), while the rest contribute zero.} Hence, the mean cross-correlation can be bounded as
\begin{align}
    \mu_{C,N}^B
    &= 
    \frac{1}{N} \left(\frac{Q_2}{N}\right) \left\lfloor \frac{Q_1}{Q_2} \right\rfloor \nonumber \\
    &< 
    \frac{1}{N} \cdot \frac{Q_2}{N} \cdot \frac{Q_1}{Q_2} \nonumber \\
    &=
    \frac{Q_1}{N^2}.
\end{align}

Since $Q_1 < N$, it follows that
\begin{align}
    \frac{Q_1}{N^2} < \frac{N}{N^2} = \frac{1}{N}.
\end{align}

Thus, we conclude that the mean cross-correlation remains strictly below \(\frac{1}{N}\) when \(N\) is an even integer, ensuring that despite the presence of nonzero overlap correlation, the constructed sequences retain a high degree of orthogonality.
\end{IEEEproof}
