\subsection{Cross-correlation of Bj\"orck Sequences}\label{appx_bjorck_cross_correlation}
% The cross-correlation of two different Bj\"orck sequences, as defined in~\eqref{eq:bjrk_cross_corr}, is given by
% \begin{align}
%     C_k = \frac{1}{P}\sum_{m=0}^{P-1} \mathbf{b}_P(m)\,\mathbf{b}_P^*((m - k) \bmod P).
% \end{align}
% This represents the correlation between a sequence \( \mathbf{b}_P(m) \) and its cyclically shifted version by \( k \) positions, where \( P \) is the sequence length. 
By expanding \( \mathbf{b}_P(m) \) and \( \mathbf{b}_P((m - k) \bmod P) \) in~\eqref{eq:bjrk_cross_corr} according to their definitions, we have
\begin{align}
    C_k = \frac{1}{P}\sum_{m=0}^{P-1} e^{j \boldsymbol{\theta}_P(m)}\,e^{-j \boldsymbol{\theta}_P((m - k) \bmod P)}.
\end{align}
This expression can be further simplified as
\begin{align}
    C_k = \frac{1}{P}\sum_{m=0}^{P-1} e^{j (\boldsymbol{\theta}_P(m) - \boldsymbol{\theta}_P((m - k) \bmod P))}.
\end{align}

Examining \(C_k\), we observe that for a zero cyclic shift (\( k = 0 \)), \( \boldsymbol{\theta}_P(m) = \boldsymbol{\theta}_P((m - k) \bmod P) \), meaning \( C_k = 1 \) when \( k = 0 \). This reflects the perfect self-correlation of the sequence with itself. For non-zero cyclic shifts (\( k \neq 0 \)), the structure of Bj\"orck sequences becomes significant. These sequences are constructed such that the phase differences \( \boldsymbol{\theta}_P(m) - \boldsymbol{\theta}_P((m - k) \bmod P) \) are uniformly distributed modulo \( 2\pi \). This uniform distribution of phase differences ensures that the terms \( e^{j (\boldsymbol{\theta}_P(m) - \boldsymbol{\theta}_P((m - k) \bmod P))} \) are evenly spread around the complex unit circle, effectively canceling each other out when summed over the sequence length \( P \). Consequently, for any non-zero shift \( k \neq 0 \), the cross-correlation \( C_k \) evaluates to zero.
