\begin{abstract}
Human-annotated data plays a vital role in training large language models (LLMs), such as supervised fine-tuning and human preference alignment. However, it is not guaranteed that paid human annotators produce high-quality data. In this paper, we study how to incentivize human annotators to do so. We start from a principal-agent model to model the dynamics between the company (the principal) and the annotator (the agent), where the principal can only monitor the annotation quality by examining $n$ samples. We investigate the maximum likelihood estimators (MLE) and the corresponding hypothesis testing to incentivize annotators: the agent is given a bonus if the MLE passes the test. By analyzing the variance of the outcome, we show that the strategic behavior of the agent makes the hypothesis testing very different from traditional ones: Unlike the exponential rate proved by the large deviation theory, the principal-agent model's hypothesis testing rate is of $\Theta(1/\sqrt{n \log n})$. Our theory implies two criteria for the \emph{golden questions} to monitor the performance of the annotators: they should be of (1) high certainty and (2) similar format to normal ones. In that light, we select a set of golden questions in human preference data. By doing incentive-compatible experiments, we find out that the annotators' behavior is better revealed by those golden questions, compared to traditional survey techniques such as instructed manipulation checks.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

Human-annotated data plays an important role in the alignment of large language models (LLMs) and other generative AI models. But probably due to the rapid pace of this field, people have not yet paid much attention to the quality control of the annotated data. The current annotation system relies on an \textit{honor code} or some cumbersome expert-based checking to ensure that the annotators are fully committed to the annotation tasks. The downstream engineering teams usually treat the annotated data as the gold standard when performing alignments on the data. Meanwhile, we see posts like the following one: 

``I'm surprised by how many low-quality jobs are out there. People tried to cheat and scam the system and then complain that they got kicked off and didn't get paid.'' 

on Reddit by PrettyFroyo7754, who claimed to have earned almost \$2000 on Outlier. In this paper, we raise and investigate the question of incentivizing high-quality human annotations from both practical and game-theoretical perspectives. 


Before we proceed, we would like to make two points on the difference and difficulty of monitoring annotators' performance in the context of LLMs. First, unlike image classification problems in which each image has a correct label, the language-related annotation task usually has no clearly correct label. Different people may have different preferences, and that is why people adopt a probability model, such as the Bradley-Terry model, to capture human preferences. This heterogeneity of human preference makes it hard to tell whether an annotator is fully committed or not. Second, the performance of each annotator cannot be evaluated through the downstream performance of the post-trained model. \cite{ananthakrishnan2024delegating,saig2024delegated} use the downstream ML model's accuracy to evaluate the annotation quality, which is infeasible for the context of LLM in which the post-training data mixes up datasets from different sources, including annotations from both human and AI.

%propose simple-form contracts to address machine learning delegation problems under various assumptions about the principals utilities, proving the (near-)optimality of these contracts. In their frameworks, agent effort is represented by the quantity of collected data, each data point is assumed to be correctly annotated, and the principals utility depends on the accuracy of the resulting machine learning model; hence, the contract is based on accuracy.

In this paper, we first propose a probability model in Section \ref{sec:setup} for the annotation process, and characterize the commitment level of the human annotator by a behavior parameter. We illustrate how the existing post-training frameworks, such as reward modeling and supervised fine-tuning, can stem from the behavioral annotation model. In Section \ref{sec:theory}, we formulate the annotation system under a principal-agent model with the two parties of the Stackelberg’s game being the LLM company and the human annotator. We develop a new and general analysis framework that uses an MLE-based hypothesis testing to monitor the annotator's performance. The analysis takes a different route than all the existing analyses of the principal-agent model, and the key is a fine-grained analysis of the estimator and the likelihood structure. Both the models in Section \ref{sec:setup} and the analyses in Section \ref{sec:theory} can be of independent interest to future works on the respective topics. Lastly, Section \ref{sec:experi} presents a reward model-based approach to construct the so-called golden questions set, which is used to monitor the performance of human annotators. We launch real social experiments on Prolific (www.prolific.com) to showcase the implementation of our proposed method. 

Our paper lies on the intersection of several streams of literature:

\textbf{Annotation monitoring and management}: There has been a stream of works in the literature of machine learning on data quality management. As aforementioned, the previous approaches that have been applied to image annotations may no longer apply here due to the heterogeneity of human preferences on language. 

\textbf{Contract design theory}: Contract design theory has been a long-studied topic in economics and computer science. The existing analyses rely on case-by-case analysis of the monitoring system, while in our paper, our analysis applies to a general class of maximum likelihood estimators. 

\textbf{Golden questions}: The introduction of golden questions has been widely used as a measure to control annotation quality in surveys and social sciences. In this paper, we will highlight how to select golden questions in the context of LLM data annotation and why it is different from other surveys.

A more detailed discussion of the related literature is deferred to Appendix \ref{app:related_lit}.

\section{Problem Setup}
\label{sec:setup}

In this section, we present the setup of human data annotation in the context of post-training large language models (LLMs). In particular, we account for the behavioral aspect of the human annotators. Our proposed model is naturally extended from the current post-training frameworks such as RLHF and DPO, and we will use it as a starting point to study the problem of how to incentivize human annotators to produce high-quality annotation data. Consider the LLM company has a dataset to be annotated, denoted as 
\[
\mathcal{S}_N \coloneqq \{s_1, \dots, s_N\}
\]
where each sample $s_i\in \mathcal{S}$ for $i=1,...,N$ is i.i.d. from some distribution $\mathcal{P}.$

Suppose the company hires one (or multiple) human annotator(s) to annotate these samples and produce an annotated dataset
$$\bar{\mathcal{S}}_N \coloneqq \{(s_1,z_1), \dots, (s_N,z_N)\}$$
where each $z_i\in\mathcal{Z}$ denotes the annotation for $s_i$ made by the annotator. To capture this annotation process, we consider the following probability model:
\begin{equation}
z_i|s_i \overset{\text{i.i.d.}}{\sim} p(\cdot;s_i,\theta_a)
\label{eqn:annotation_model}
\end{equation}
where the function $p(\cdot; s, \theta) \in \Delta^{\mathcal{Z}}$ specifies the conditional distribution of $z$ under $s$ and $\theta$. Here $\theta_a \in \Theta$ denotes an annotator-specific \textit{behavioral} parameter that relates simultaneously to the commitment level of the annotator and the quality of the annotated labels. In other words, $\theta_a$ captures the behavioral heterogeneity among the annotators, and admittedly (though often ignored in the current post-training practice), different annotators may have different commitment levels. In this sense, the function $p(\cdot;\cdot, \cdot)$ maps the unlabeled $s_i$ and the parameter $\theta_a$ to a distribution over the annotation space $\mathcal{Z}$.

Importantly, the parameter $\theta_a$ is the effort/commitment level determined by the annotator but cannot be observed by the LLM company. Therefore, the evaluation of the human annotator's performance, or equivalently, the evaluation of the annotated dataset $\bar{\mathcal{S}}_N$'s quality, hinges on the estimation of $\theta_a$. And the mechanism design of the annotation payment system should drive the annotator to choose a $\theta_a$ desirable for the LLM company.

\subsection{Existing post-training frameworks under the behavior model \eqref{eqn:annotation_model}} 

\label{sec:examples}
To better generate intuitions, we illustrate the model \eqref{eqn:annotation_model} under two specific contexts -- preference alignment and supervised fine-tuning (SFT).

\textbf{Pairwise preference data annotation.}

The task of pairwise preference annotation concerns collecting data samples in the following format:
    \[(s,z)=(x,y_1,y_2,Z)\]
where the unlabeled sample $s=(x,y_1,y_2)$ that consists of $x$ as the prompt and $y_1, y_2$ as two candidate responses to $x$, and the annotated label $z=Z\in\mathcal{Z}$ is a random variable that denotes the annotator's preference between $y_1$ and $y_2$.

In the canonical setup of reward modeling (\cite{bai2022training, ouyang2022training} among others), the label $Z$ takes binary values with $\mathcal{Z} = \{0, 1\}$, indicating if $y_1$ is preferred over $y_2$ in the context of $x$. Furthermore, it assumes $Z$ is a Bernoulli random variable such that
\begin{equation}
\mathbb{P}(y_1 \succ y_2\ |\ x) = \mathbb{P}(Z=1|x,y_1,y_2) = 1-\mathbb{P}(Z=0|x,y_1,y_2).   
\label{eqn:Z_label}
\end{equation}
This assumption has been (sometimes unconsciously) used as the backbone of training mainstream preference and reward models; some literature (e.g., \cite{gao2024impact,liang2024robust,wang2024secrets}) questions the assumption and discusses the case of noisy or poisoned labels of $Z$ without \eqref{eqn:Z_label}. These existing works study how one can mitigate the effect of the noisy labels (violation of \eqref{eqn:Z_label}) in the downstream alignment of LLMs. In comparison, we inject a behavioral element into \eqref{eqn:Z_label} and aim to investigate the problem of how to incentivize the human annotators to meet the condition \eqref{eqn:Z_label}. 

Specifically, we consider two natural variants of \eqref{eqn:Z_label}, both of which serve as special cases of the general annotation model \eqref{eqn:annotation_model}.

\textit{Variant I (latent-factor model for human annotation)}: We denote $p^*(x,y_1,y_2)\coloneqq \mathbb{P}(y_1 \succ y_2\ |\ x)$. The latent factor model uses a binary latent variable $V\in\{0,1\}$ to denote the two modes of the annotator. When $V=0$, the annotator randomly assigns the label $Z$; when $V=1$, the annotator is fully committed and labels $Z$ following the ideal value $p^\ast(x, y_1, y_2)$.
    \begin{align*}
        \mathbb{P}(Z=1|V=0, x, y_1, y_2) &= \mathbb{P}(Z=0|V=0, x, y_1, y_2) = \frac{1}{2},\\
        \mathbb{P}(Z=1|V=1, x, y_1, y_2) &= 1 - \mathbb{P}(Z=0|V=1, x, y_1, y_2) = p^\ast(x, y_1, y_2).
    \end{align*}
    The probability of $V=1$ is specified by the parameter $\theta_a \in \Theta = [0,1]$:
    \[
        \mathbb{P}(V=1|x, y_1, y_2) \equiv \theta_a \text{ \ for all \ } (x, y_1, y_2).
    \]
The above jointly specifies $p(\cdot;s_i,\theta_a)$ in \eqref{eqn:annotation_model}, i.e., how $z_i$ is generated based on $s_i = (x_i,y_{i1},y_{i2})$. The ideal case \eqref{eqn:Z_label} corresponds to $\theta_a=1$, and an effective incentivizing scheme of the annotation system should encourage the annotators to choose the value of $\theta_a$ close to 1. The model is studied in \cite{liu2025humans} to develop a self-consistency check to monitor the performance of human annotations. 

\textit{Variant II -- Bradley-Terry model with temperature:} The Bradley-Terry model \cite{bradley1952rank} takes one step further from \eqref{eqn:Z_label} and assumes there exists an oracle reward score $r^*(x,y)$ for every prompt response pair $(x,y).$ The following probability distribution naturally relaxes the Bradley-Terry model to a behavioral setting where the temperature parameter $\theta_a$ reflects the annotators' commitment level. 
\[
p(z = 1;s,\theta_a) = \mathbb{P}(Z=1|x,y_1,y_2, \theta_a) = \bigg(1+\exp\Big(-\theta_a \cdot \big(r^\ast(x,y_1) - r^\ast(x,y_2)\big)\Big)\bigg)^{-1},
\]
where $s=(x,y_1,y_2)$ is the sample to be annotated, and the parameter $\theta_a\in\Theta=[0,1]$. In this way, the above expression also serves as an example of the general model \eqref{eqn:annotation_model}. The idealized Bradley-Terry model corresponds to $\theta_a = 1$, while at the other end of the spectrum, the annotator randomly labels when $\theta_a = 0$. $\theta_a$ can be viewed as the inverse temperature; the smaller $\theta$, the noisier the data becomes. An effective annotation system should encourage the annotation to choose $\theta_a$ close to 1.

\begin{comment}
