    \[
        p(d;\theta_a) = p(w; \theta_a) = \frac{1+(2 p^\ast(x, y_1, y_2) - 1)\cdot \theta_a}{2}.
    \]
\end{comment}


\textbf{SFT data annotation.} 

When people collect data for SFT, the unannotated data $s_i$ represents the prompt $x_i$, and the annotator provides a response $z_i$ as the annotation. This also applies to the case of preference data annotation where the annotator is provided an editing option to further improve upon the preferred response (for example, as in Llama 3 \cite{grattafiori2024llama}). In such scenarios, one can model the annotation quality by assuming the quality of the provided response $z_i$ follows a normal distribution $r(s_i,z_i) \sim \mathcal{N}(\theta_a, \sigma^2)$ where $\theta_a\in \Theta = [\underline{\theta},\bar{\theta}]$ denotes the commitment level of the annotator and the function $r(\cdot,\cdot)$ can be viewed as some evaluation function that measures the quality of the response $z_i.$ Here, as the previous two examples, an effective system should encourage the human annotator to choose a $\theta_a$ as large as possible, i.e., a higher quality of the provided response.

The above three examples can be of independent interest in future studies of the behavioral aspects of human annotations. For the remainder of the paper, we will work on the general model \eqref{eqn:annotation_model}.

\subsection{Golden question and annotation system}

The LLM company considers a monitoring dataset of \textit{golden questions} \cite{le2010ensuring}
\[
\mathcal{D}_n \coloneqq \{d_1, \dots, d_n\}.
\]
where each $d_j = (s_{N+j}, z_{N+j})$ denotes one annotated sample of the same format as those in $\bar{\mathcal{S}}_N$. These golden questions can be viewed as samples that are most effective in assessing the performance of the annotators. To select these golden questions, one can sample each $d_j = (s_{N+j}, z_{N+j})$ randomly from the annotated dataset $\bar{\mathcal{S}}_N$. Alternatively, one can also insert some pre-selected samples into the to-be-annotated dataset $\mathcal{S}_N$, and use these samples and their annotations as the set $\mathcal{D}_n$. Generally, it does no hurt to think of $\mathcal{D}_n$ as a ``subset'' of $\bar{\mathcal{S}}_N$.
Notation-wise, we write
\begin{equation}
d_j \overset{\text{i.i.d.}}{\sim} p(\cdot \,; \theta_a), \quad \text{for } j=1, \dots, n.
\label{eqn:monitoring_data}
\end{equation}
Here, the distribution is with respect to the joint distribution of $s_{N+j}$ and $z_{N+j}$.


Then the annotation system reduces to two parts: 
\begin{itemize}
\item Quality monitoring: estimate the unobserved $\theta_a$ via $\mathcal{D}_n$ and denote the estimator as $\hat{\theta}_a$.
\item Payment design: pay the annotator based on $\hat{\theta}_a$.
\end{itemize}
In Section \ref{sec:theory}, we analyze the dynamics of such an annotation system. In Section \ref{sec:experi}, we discuss how to select the golden questions set $\mathcal{D}_n$.

