\section{Dynamics between Annotator and Company}
\label{sec:theory}

We use the classic principal-agent model \citep{harris1979optimal, holmstrom1979moral, grossman1992analysis, laffont2009theory} to study the dynamics between the company and the annotator \citep{bates2022principal, ananthakrishnan2024delegating, saig2024incentivizing}.
The model is to capture the information asymmetry between the principal and the agent. It is a ``leader-follower'' game (also known as Stackelberg's game), where the principal (the company) moves first and the agent (the annotator) moves after observing the principal's move. The principal (the company) wants a task (e.g., data annotation) to be accomplished and pays the agent (the annotator) to do so. However, the company cannot observe the agent's \emph{action} $\theta_a$; the agent behaves according to their own benefits, which may hurt the total welfare (also known as the moral hazard). In the context of LLM data annotation, the annotator chooses the commitment level $\theta_a$, which is unobservable to the company. To mitigate this issue, the principal must (1) \emph{monitor} the action of the agent by some methods (e.g., sample some data to examine the quality); and (2) offer some payment scheme (also known as the contract) to \emph{incentivize} the annotator to behave properly. In this paper, we consider the maximum-likelihood estimator (MLE):
\begin{equation*}
    \hat{\theta}_{n}^{\text{MLE}} \coloneqq \max_{\theta \in \Theta} \, \sum_{i=1}^n \log\left(p(d_j; \theta)\right)
\end{equation*}
where $\mathcal{D}_n=\{d_1,...,d_n\}$ is the golden question set and the likelihood function $p$ is as in \eqref{eqn:monitoring_data}. We omit the $n$ and ``MLE'' for simplicity if there is no notational confusion. 


\begin{algorithm}[ht!]
\centering
\caption{Binary contract}
\label{alg_binary_contr}
\begin{algorithmic}
\State \textbf{Input:} An MLE $\hat{\theta}(\mathcal{D}_n)$ and a binary contract $F_n = (\Psi, W_0, W_b)$
\State \textcolor{blue}{\%\% $\Psi(\hat{\theta}) = \mathbf{1}\{\hat{\theta} \notin \mathcal{R}_n\}$ represents some test with reject region $\mathcal{R}_n$}
\State \textcolor{blue}{\%\% $W_0$ is the base salary and $W_b$ is the bonus for passing the performance assessment}
\State Perform the test $\Psi(\hat{\theta}(\mathcal{D}_n))$
\If{$\Psi(\hat{\theta}(\mathcal{D}_n))=0$, i.e., the annotator fails the performance test}
\State The company pays the annotator $w_a = W_0$
\Else
\State  The company pays the annotator $w_a = W_0+W_b$
\EndIf
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

Algorithm \ref{alg_binary_contr} describes a practical payment system based on the MLE. The key of the algorithm relies on a test $\Psi$, an indicator function based on whether $\hat{\theta}$ falls out of the reject region $\mathcal{R}_n \subset \Theta$:
%The principal uses the MLE as evidence to pay the agent a certain amount of money. As for the payment scheme, we are interested in the binary contract, where the payment depends on a hypothesis testing $\Psi$'s result. 
\begin{equation}
    \Psi(\hat{\theta}) \coloneqq \mathbf{1} \left\{ \hat{\theta} \notin \mathcal{R}_n \right\}.
\end{equation}
If $\Psi(\hat{\theta}) = 1$, the agent receives an additional bonus $W_b$; otherwise, the agent only gets the base $W_0$. This binary payment structure is known as the binary contract; we will analyze its properties in this section and analyze the alternative of the linear contract in the Appendix. 

\begin{comment}
Following the tradition of the principal-agent model \citep{harris1979optimal, holmstrom1979moral, grossman1992analysis, laffont2009theory, frick2023monitoring}, we analyze the risk-averse case, where the loss suffered is of the same order as the payment's variance $\mathrm{Var}(w_a)$ (or equivalently, the same order as the variance of $\Psi$ under the constraint that $\theta_a(F_n) = \theta^\ast$) \citep{laffont2009theory, frick2023monitoring, liu2025humans}:
\begin{equation}
    \mathcal{C} - \mathcal{C}_n = \Theta(\mathrm{Var}(\Psi)).
\end{equation}
We aim to characterize the (best) convergence rate of $\mathrm{Var}(w_a)$ as the sample number $n$ grows to infinity.


Classical hypothesis testing problem treats the agent's action $\theta_a$ and the reject region $\mathcal{R}_n$ as priors independent of the contract $F_n$, where one can adopt the large deviation theory tools to show that $\mathrm{Var}(\Psi) = \exp(-\Theta(n))$. 
% See \cite{frick2023monitoring} for an example; their discrete $\Theta$ case keeps $\theta_a \equiv \theta^\ast$ for large enough $n$'s. 
However, in the problem of the principal-agent model, the agent's action $\theta_a(F_n)$ is a consequence of $F_n$; it is strategic. As $n$ grows, $\theta_a$ (or $F_n$) may change accordingly. Such an involved dynamics precludes the classical large deviation theory tools and remains an open problem \cite{frick2023monitoring}.
In this section, we answer the problem with a mini-max optimal rate of
\[
\mathrm{Var}(\Psi) = \Theta\left(\frac{1}{\sqrt{n \log n}}\right)
\]
under some non-restrictive regularity conditions of the distribution $p(d; \theta)$. Our analysis is the first of this kind, which itself is of independent interest.
\end{comment}

\subsection{Principal-agent model for data annotation}

\label{sec:pa_models}
% Since we are considering the continuous action space $\Theta$, we assume all the following functions are at least twice continuously differentiable.

The analysis of the dynamics between the principal and the agent begins with a formal definition of the utilities of both parties. 

\begin{comment}
Recall that the agent takes some action $\theta_a \in \Theta$ to annotate $N$ to-be-annotated samples $\mathcal{S}_N$ and $n$ monitoring samples $\mathcal{D}_n$, receiving $w_a$ units of wealth:
\[
w_a = W_0 + W_b \cdot \Psi(\hat{\theta}).
\]
Such information is summarized as a \emph{contract} $F_n = (\Psi, W_0, W_b)$.   
\end{comment}


To capture how the agent decides their action $\theta_a$, the utility function of the agent is modeled by
\[
U_a(w_a, \theta) \coloneqq G_a(w_a) - E(\theta).
\]
Here, $w_a$ is the payment defined by Algorithm \ref{alg_binary_contr}, and $G_a(w_a)$ is the monetary utility of the agent which models the agent's nature as risk-averse/neutral/seeking. The effort function $E:\Theta \rightarrow \mathbb{R}$ is a function of $\theta$, characterizing the disutility (or the effort) of the agent's action. 

Meanwhile, the principal's utility is given by:
\[
U_p(w_a, \theta) \coloneqq -w_a + \mu(\theta).
\]
The function $\mu: \Theta \rightarrow \mathbb{R}$ models the data utility concerning the quality $\theta$.
Now we can formulate the dynamics between the annotator and the company as a constrained optimization problem. As a benchmark, the idealized scenario is called the \textit{first-best}, which is
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{C} \coloneqq \max_{F_n, \theta \in \Theta} \, \mathbb{E}\left[U_p(w_a, \theta)\right], \ \ 
\text{s.t. }  \mathbb{E}\left[U_a(w_a, \theta)\right] \geq U_0.
\label{eq:first_best}
\end{equation}
Here, the expectation is taken for the randomness of the monitoring result $\Psi(\hat{\theta})$. The right-hand side of the constraint $U_0$ is called the leisure utility, representing the utility the agent gains by rejecting the contract (e.g., to work for another company). This constraint is referred to as the \textit{individual rationality} (IR). We denote the optimal solution of $\theta$ in this first-best problem by $\theta^\ast$. 


The first-best is an idealized scenario where both the contract $F_n$ and the annotation quality $\theta$ are fully controlled by the principal. %It can be achieved if the principal has a perfect monitoring system and designs contracts $\Psi(\hat{\theta}) = \mathbf{1}\{\hat{\theta} = \theta^\prime\}$ for any desirable $\theta^\prime$. 
However, in reality, we can only hope for a second-best as the action $\theta$ is taken by the agent; the first-best is only a benchmark. Specifically, the practical setting (known as the \emph{second-best}) is where the agent decides the action $\theta_a$ maximizing $\mathbb{E}[U_a]$, formulated as the following bi-level optimization problem 
\begin{equation}
\begin{split}
\mathcal{C}_n \coloneqq \max_{F_n} \, & \mathbb{E}\left[U_p(w_a, \theta_a(F_n))\right], \\
\text{s.t. } & \mathbb{E}\left[U_a(w_a, \theta_a(F_n))\right] \geq U_0, \ \  \theta_a(F_n) \in \argmax_{\theta \in \Theta} \, \mathbb{E}\left[U_a(w_a, \theta)\right].
\end{split}
\label{eq:second_best}
\end{equation}
Compared to \eqref{eq:first_best}, there is an extra constraint in \eqref{eq:second_best}, which says that the annotator decides the action by maximizing their expected utility under the contract $F_n$, also known as the \textit{incentive compatibility} (IC). That is, $\theta_a$ is a function of $F_n$. And the principal cannot control the agent's action directly, thus the outer program \eqref{eq:second_best} has only one decision variable $F_n$ for the principal.


As an intuition, the principal can increase the sample amount $n$ to close the gap between $\hat{\theta}$ and $\theta_a$, and then design the contract $F_n$ in a way to incentivize $\theta_a$ to move towards $\theta^*$. This leads to a smaller gap between the second-best and the idealized first-best. However, different from a pure statistical estimation problem of analyzing the convergence rate of $\hat{\theta}$ to $\theta_a$, the principal-agent model is more involved: as $n$ increases, the agent's strategic action $\theta_a$ may vary accordingly.


Before we present the main results, we introduce some commonly adopted assumptions \citep{frick2023monitoring, liu2025humans} on the utility functions here.
\begin{assumption}
\label{assum:regular_PA_model}
We assume
    \begin{enumerate}[label=(\alph*)]
        \item Agent: All the functions ($G_a, E, \mu$) are twice continuously differentiable. The agent action space $\Theta$ is a continuous bounded subset of $\mathbb{R}^k$, and $\theta^\ast \in \mathrm{interior}(\Theta)$ with $\theta^\ast \notin \argmin_{\theta \in \Theta} E(\theta)$. The agent is risk-averse with $\frac{\mathrm{d}^2 G_a}{\mathrm{d} w_a ^{\ 2}} < 0$; and the effort function is convex (that is, the marginal effort increases) with 
        $\frac{\partial^2 E}{\partial \theta^2} \succeq 0$. Furthermore, the wealth needed to compensate for the effort is a strongly convex function of $\theta$, that is, for any $u \geq 0$, $\frac{\partial^2}{\partial \theta^2}\left(G_a^{-1}(E(\theta) + u)\right) \succ 0$;
        \item Principal: The principal's data utility function is concave (that is, the marginal data quality utility decreases) with
        $\frac{\partial^2 \mu}{\partial \theta^2} \preceq 0$;
        \item Bounded payment: The paid wealth (or the wage) $w_a$ is almost surely bounded. That is, there exist constants $\underline{w}, \overline{w}\in \mathbb{R}$ such that
        $w_a \in [\underline{w}, \overline{w}]$ almost surely for any $F_n$. Besides, the wage range is rich enough to compensate any level of effort (plus the leisure utility), i.e.
        $E(\Theta) + U_0 \subset \mathrm{interior}\left(G_a([\underline{w}, \overline{w}])\right)$.
    \end{enumerate}
\end{assumption}
\begin{remark}
For Part (a), the risk-averse nature of the agent is commonly assumed when analyzing the principal-agent model. It should be justified by two reasons: First, the risk-averse nature of human is validated by empirical studies \citep{kahneman2013prospect,ruggeri2020replicating}; second, if the agent is risk-neutral (linear $G_a$), most discussions of the principal-agent model become trivial \citep{harris1979optimal}, where one single evaluation sample is enough and there is no loss caused by the risk \cite{ananthakrishnan2024delegating}. The assumption of the convexity of the effort function $E$ is to model human nature, where a higher level of effort has a higher marginal effort, and we need to compensate the agent at a larger rate when the agent is already at a high performance. For Part (b), The concavity of $\mu$ is widely acknowledged and verified by the downstream performance of a machine learning model or a large language model under noisy data \citep{gao2024impact,chowdhury2024provably,liang2024robust}. We do not specify the exact form of $\mu$ but only assume the concavity, in contrast to \cite{ananthakrishnan2024delegating}, where the authors assume some power function as the utility concerning the annotated number $N$. Although the scaling law of $N$ of the large language model data, such as the reward modeling \citep{gao2023scaling}, has been studied, it still remains unclear in which exact form the utility function would be for the annotation quality. Part (c) is only to ensure that the agent's optimal strategy (which must be a unique pure strategy due to the strong convexity combining Parts (a) and (b)) exists.
\end{remark}






