\subsection{Main result}


We now present our main result, which solves the problem via a novel 
and more general analysis compared to all the existing literature. Before we proceed, we introduce some regulatory conditions on the likelihood function. It is easy to verify that the examples in Section \ref{sec:examples} all meet these assumptions. 
\begin{assumption}
\label{assum:regulating_likelihood}
    \begin{enumerate}[label=(\alph*)]
        \item  We assume that any directional derivative of the log-likelihood function (that is, $\left\langle \frac{\partial}{\partial \theta} \log p(d_j; \theta), v \right \rangle$ for some unit vector $v$) is of positive definite variance and finite third moment with respect to the randomness of $d_j$ for every $\theta \in \Theta$. That is, for any unit vector $v$ and $\theta \in \Theta$,
        \[
        \mathrm{Var}_{d_j}\left(\left\langle\frac{\partial}{\partial \theta} \log p(d_j; \theta), v\right\rangle\right) > 0, \, \mathbb{E}_{d_j}\left[\left|\left\langle\frac{\partial}{\partial \theta} \log p(d_j; \theta), v\right\rangle\right|^3\right] < \infty.
        \]
        % Furthermore, denote the maximum variance at $\theta^\ast$ by $\mathcal{V}$:
        % \[
        % \mathcal{V} \coloneqq \max_v\, \mathrm{Var}_{d_j}\left(\left\langle\frac{\partial}{\partial \theta} \log p(d_j; \theta), v\right\rangle\right).
        % \]
        \item We assume that the likelihood $p(d_j;\theta)$ is $\alpha$-strongly log-concave as a function of $\theta$ for any realization of each $d_j$ almost surely. Furthermore, we assume that the negative log-likelihood is $\beta$-strongly smooth. That is, there exist constants $\alpha, \beta > 0$ such that for any eigenvalue $\lambda$ of $\frac{\partial^2}{\partial \theta^2} \log p(d_j; \theta)$ satisfies that $-\lambda \in [\alpha, \beta]$ almost surely.
    \end{enumerate}
\end{assumption}


