\begin{theorem}
\label{thm:binary}
Under Assumptions \ref{assum:regular_PA_model} and \ref{assum:regulating_likelihood}, there exists some $F_n$ such that $\theta_a(F_n) = \theta^\ast$ with
\[
\mathrm{Var}(\Psi) = \mathcal{O}\left(\frac{1}{\sqrt{n \log n}}\right).
\]
Furthermore, such a rate is mini-max optimal: we can construct some example satisfying Assumptions \ref{assum:regular_PA_model} and \ref{assum:regulating_likelihood} such that for any $F_n$ with $\theta_a(F_n) = \theta^\ast$,
\[
\inf_{F_n} \, \mathrm{Var}(\Psi) = \Omega\left(\frac{1}{\sqrt{n \log n}}\right).
\]
\end{theorem}
We make the following remarks about Theorem \ref{thm:binary}: The requirement of $\theta_a(F_n) = \theta^\ast$ is to prevent naive reduction of the payment variance: the principal can simply set the reject region $\mathcal{R}_n = \emptyset$ to make $\mathrm{Var}(w_a) = \mathrm{Var}(\Psi) = 0$, but it does not incentivize the agent to behave properly. We set up the benchmark behavior $\theta^\ast$ to exclude those possibilities. The requirement is non-restrictive when upper bounding $\mathcal{C} - \mathcal{C}_n$ since adding one more restriction $\theta_a(F_n) = \theta^\ast$ only decreases the possible payoff in the second-best \eqref{eq:second_best} (which will be further discussed in Appendix \ref{subsec:proof_converge_to_optimal_theta}). As a direct consequence, we have a mini-max optimal bound for the first-best second-best gap (where the lower bound is guaranteed by Theorem 4.6 of \cite{liu2025humans}):
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{C} - \mathcal{C}_n = \mathcal{O}\left(\frac{1}{\sqrt{n \log n}}\right).
\label{eqn:first_second_gap}
\end{equation}


In traditional hypothesis testing problems, the agent's action is viewed as a \emph{prior} independent of $F_n$ (for example, $\theta_a \equiv \theta^\ast$). If $\theta^\ast$ remains a distance $d(\theta^\ast, \mathcal{R}_n) > 0$ to the reject region, then the convergence rate of the variance is of exponential rate according to the large deviation theory (analogous to Theorem 1 of \cite{frick2023monitoring}), where
\[
\mathrm{Var}(\Psi) = \exp\left(- n \cdot \inf_{\theta \in \mathcal{R}_n, E(\theta) < E(\theta^\ast)} D_{\mathrm{kl}}\big(p(d; \theta) \| p(d; \theta^\ast)\big) + o(n)\right).
\]
However, in the real cases, the agent's action $\theta_a$ is strategic as a function $\theta_a(F_n)$. Intuitively, if the agent takes some fixed action $\theta \notin \mathcal{R}_n$, the bonus probability $\mathbb{P}(\Psi(\hat{\theta}) = 1)$ will converge fast to 1 as $n$ grows large, leading to a better payoff. The agent will utilize this observation to maximize the utility by ``shrinking'' the effort level $\theta$ accordingly: the effort can be saved such that even though $\mathbb{P}(\Psi(\hat{\theta}) = 1)$ slightly decreases, the overall utility is better. In that sense, for any fixed $\mathcal{R}_n$, the action $\theta_a(F_n)$ is converging to the boundary of $\mathcal{R}_n$ as $n$ grows to infinity; or if we want to keep $\theta_a(F_n)$ at some fixed level (say $\theta^\ast$), the reject region $\mathcal{R}_n$ (and the wages $W_0$ and $W_b$) should adjust accordingly. No matter which case, the rate function $\inf_{\theta \in \mathcal{R}_n, E(\theta) < E(\theta_a)} D_{\mathrm{kl}}\big(p(d; \theta) \| p(d; \theta_a)\big)$ is now vanishing. As a consequence, previous tools no longer apply. In this paper, we answer the problem of $\mathrm{Var}(\Psi)$ under the principal-agent model with a novel analysis that, unlike the traditional hypothesis testing, the convergence rate is much worse due to the strategic behavior of the agent. 


Our result generalizes previous studies and is the first one to analyze the general MLE as the testing instrument in the principal-agent framework. \cite{liu2025humans} only constructs a very specific binomial distribution and derives a hand-crafted analysis using the properties of the binomial distribution. Our analysis is based on the central limit theorem to transform any kind of MLE to an asymptotically normal distribution. The core of the analysis is to curve the incentives $\frac{\partial}{\partial \theta} \mathbb{P}(\Psi(\hat{\theta}) = 1)$. We use the log-trick, turning the incentives into a partial moment estimation problem. Then we adopt Berry-Esseen-type bounds \citep{berry1941accuracy, esseen1942liapunov, nagaev1965some, michel1981constant} to analyze the convergence rate to the normal distribution. In one word, our analysis captures the essence of the phenomenon (due to CLT and the properties of the normal distribution). 

