\subsection{Intuitions for practice}

We briefly discuss what our theory implies for the practice here:
In our theoretical bounds (both Theorem \ref{thm:linear_contract} and Theorem \ref{thm:binary}), the risk has the property that as $n$ grows large, the monitoring result becomes more precise, reducing the overall loss. Such an observation is due to the risk-averse nature of the agent \cite{harris1979optimal}. Another important fact is that the risk scales linearly with the variance of the annotation ($\mathcal{V}$) over $\mathcal{D}_n$. It provides a key insight that we need the evaluation metric to be clear and precise enough to prevent confusion, which brings up our following design of golden questions. The golden questions should bear low variance when the annotator fully commits. 


Apart from the above observations concerning the performance bounds, we note that a vital point should be kept in mind: the monitoring dataset $\mathcal{D}_n$ should share the same annotation quality as the to-be-annotated dataset $\mathcal{W}_n$ (Assumption \ref{assum:same_action}). If one is to sample $n$ out of $N$ to construct the dataset $\mathcal{D}_n$, then the assumption is automatically fulfilled. However, if one is to use some golden questions with known answers, the design of the golden questions should be very careful: the golden questions should not be easily recognized from the normal ones by the annotator. Otherwise, the annotator can easily get away from the monitoring by putting in higher efforts only on the golden questions while neglecting the normal ones. In that case, the monitoring method will be less powerful or even vague.
