\section{Golden Questions: Effectiveness and Selection Criteria}

\label{sec:experi}
\begin{comment}

\begin{example}[Latent factor model and golden questions]
    In the previous example, computing the likelihood needs the knowledge of $p^\ast$, which may be too strong. If we set up a different dataset $\mathcal{D}_n$ from the to-be-annotated $\mathcal{W}_n$ with \emph{known answers} and \emph{clear criteria}, we can use those questions to monitor the annotation quality. For example, we can construct questions by the traditional attention-check items, such as ``Please select the first option in this question.'' or by selecting some very certain questions (which we call ``golden questions''). We assume that these questions are answered correctly if the annotator is committed $V=1$ with probability $1$ and are of correct probability $\frac{1}{2}$ if $V=0$. The overall correct probability follows that
    \[
    p(d;\theta_a) = \frac{\theta_a + 1}{2}.
    \]
    The self-consistency check in \cite{liu2025humans} can be regarded as a special case of this setting.
\end{example}
\end{comment}

In this section, we discuss how to select the golden questions set $\mathcal{D}_n$ and verify the effectiveness of the selection criteria via real social experiments. 
% First, if we carefully examine the bound in Theorem \ref{thm:binary}, it implies that we should select $\mathcal{D}_n$ in a way to reduce the variance of the MLE estimator $\hat{\theta}$. In the context of preference data annotation, it corresponds to samples that have objective and unambiguous answers, and these will be the ones most effective in estimating the agent's commitment level. 
From the theoretical side, the theoretical model in the previous sections has two implications for selecting the golden questions: 
First, the golden questions should have certain answers. We cannot directly access each annotator's behavioral model; we can only use a model \eqref{eqn:monitoring_data} to represent the overall behaviors. If the golden questions are very uncertain, then different annotators' opinions will make their behavioral model very different from the population-level model \eqref{eqn:monitoring_data}. That is also true from the practical side: if a golden question has an uncertain answer -- say, a 50/50 split in the correctness of two responses -- it is hard to tell whether an annotator chooses thoughtfully or simply randomly (see examples in Appendix~\ref{appx:example}), bringing up ambiguities when the company gives different levels of wages according to those questions. 
Second, the golden questions should take the same format and follow the same distribution as the remaining samples in $\mathcal{S}_n$. This will ensure a consistent behavior of the annotator on $\mathcal{D}_n$ and $\mathcal{S}_n$, and thus make $\mathcal{D}_n$ a valid monitoring dataset. We refer to more discussions in Appendix \ref{subsec:further_implications}.

%\paragraph{Golden question criteria}\ Theorem \ref{thm:binary} indicates that the risk scales linearly with the variance of the annotation ($\mathcal{V}$) over the evaluation set $\mathcal{D}_n$, suggesting that golden questions should have a clearly defined, unambiguous correct answer to provide a clear and convincing evaluation system. Furthermore, our model \eqref{eqn:monitoring_data} requires that annotators behave consistently across both golden and non-golden sets. These two principles indeed align with widely accepted criteria in the literature \cite{oleson2011programmatic,checco2018all,muszynski2023attention}, which propose that good golden questions should: (i) have objective, non-ambiguous answers, and (ii) resemble the non-golden questions in style and content. Intuitively, the first criterion ensures that annotators are incentivized to respond carefully. For example, if a golden question has an uncertain answer—say, a 50/50 split in the correctness of two responses—there is little incentive for an annotator to choose thoughtfully over guessing. The second criterion prevents annotators from gaming the evaluation system. If golden questions are easily identifiable, annotators may simply focus on answering those correctly while neglecting the rest.

In the light of these two aspects, a practical implementation of the golden question monitoring system entails a moderate size of $\mathcal{D}_n$ with $n$ being a few tens or up to a hundred questions. Otherwise, a scarcity of golden questions can leave evaluation systems vulnerable to attack by annotators \cite{checco2018all}. Algorithm \ref{alg:high_certainty_selection} gives a scalable algorithm for automatic construction of $\mathcal{D}_n.$ The idea is simple, and it relies on a certainty estimation function $c$ to select samples from $\mathcal{S}_N$ where the ambiguity of the annotation is minimal.

%\paragraph{Creation for preference data}\  Creating golden questions that meet both criteria is particularly challenging in the context of LLMs. In supervised fine-tuning (SFT) data, ground-truth answers are often unavailable due to the open-ended nature of responses. The same challenge applies to pairwise preference data, where the distinction between two responses can be subtle or subjective. These challenges make manual selection of high-quality golden questions both difficult and labor-intensive. Nevertheless, incorporating more golden questions is desirable. As Theorem~\ref{thm:binary} suggests, increasing the number of golden questions $n$ leads to more precise monitoring, thereby reducing overall loss due to the risk-averse nature of the agent \cite{harris1979optimal}. Furthermore, a To address this challenge, we propose an automated method for generating golden questions in Algorithm \ref{alg:high_certainty_selection}.

\begin{algorithm}[ht!]
\caption{Certainty-based Golden Question Selection}
\label{alg:high_certainty_selection}
\begin{algorithmic}
\State \textbf{Input:} Unannotated data $\mathcal{S}_N = \{s_i\}_{i=1}^N$, certainty estimator $c(\cdot)$, golden questions number $n$
\State Compute $c(s_i)$ for each $s_i\in \mathcal{S}_N$
\State Select the top-$n$ certainty samples (with largest $c(s_i)$) as the golden questions and \textbf{return}
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

Algorithm~\ref{alg:high_certainty_selection} basically reduces the selection of golden questions to the certainty estimator $c$. For the context of preference data annotation, one natural choice for $c$ is to use the probability elicited from a well-trained reward model (RM). Specifically, as described in Section \ref{sec:examples}, each sample to be annotated consists of a tuple $s=(x,y_1,y_2).$ Then one can choose the certainty estimator $c(s)=|\hat{\mathbb{P}}(y_1 \succ y_2\ |\ x)-1/2|$ where $\hat{\mathbb{P}}$ is the estimated preference probability calculated based on an RM. A larger value of $c(s)$ indicates the RM is more certain about this sample. Such an implementation meets the two points mentioned above: (i) the samples selected (very likely) have unambiguous answers; and (ii) the samples are similar to the remainder of the dataset $\mathcal{S}_N$.

To validate the RM-based selection, we perform an experiment as Figure \ref{fig:Accuracy_SkyGemma} using the Skywork’s reward model \cite{liu2024skywork} and six preference datasets. It reports the prediction accuracy defined as the percentage of samples whose predicted preferences match human annotations across multiple preference datasets. For most datasets, accuracy on the top-$10$ percent most certain samples exceeds $90$ percent. This high agreement not only demonstrates that these samples are unambiguous (otherwise, even a perfectly human-aligned reward model would struggle to predict the real annotation due to the inherent ambiguity) but also confirms that the reward model reliably recovers the true preference labels. In Appendix \ref{subapx:data_describ_extensions}, we observe similar trends as in Figure \ref{fig:Accuracy_SkyGemma} across different reward models, confirming that the conclusions are robust to the choice of the reward model. Further details on the datasets and experimental setup are also provided in Appendix \ref{subapx:data_describ_extensions}. We note that the practical implementation of this RM-based selection can be combined with human judgment; we use the RM to pre-screen and select a candidate set of golden questions, and then employ some human experts to further filter out the final golden questions set. 

%Algorithm~\ref{alg:high_certainty_selection} relies on a certainty estimator $c(\cdot)$ that quantifies the certainty of the model in its preference for one response over another. For a sample $s = (x, y_1, y_2)$, the true certainty is measured as $|\mathbb{P}(y_1 \succ y_2\ |\ x)-0.5|$ where $\mathbb{P}(y_1 \succ y_2 \mid x)$ is the estimated probability that $y_1$ is preferred over $y_2$. A larger value of $c(s)$ should indicate higher certainty that one response is better than the other. When $c(\cdot)$ can accurately estimate the certainty, Algorithm \ref{alg:high_certainty_selection} ensures that selected golden questions: (i) have (relatively) non-ambiguous answers due to high preference certainty, and (ii) closely resemble non-golden questions, as they are drawn from the same dataset.

%The effectiveness of Algorithm~\ref{alg:high_certainty_selection} depends on the quality of the certainty estimator. Ideally, $c(s)$ would exactly equal the true certainty $|\mathbb{P}(y_1 \succ y_2 \mid x)-0.5|$. This is unattainable in practice, but we can leverage some open-source reward models to approximate the true preference probability $\mathbb{P}(y_1 \succ y_2 \mid x)$ (and consequently the true certainty). The following experiment shows that on the high-certainty samples, reward models align well with human preference. 

