\textbf{Social experiments}\ We conduct real social experiments on Prolific (www.prolific.com) to examine how human annotator behavior differs depending on the ``quality'' of golden questions included in the annotation task. Specifically, we compare two types of golden questions on a preference dataset \cite{ji2024pku}:  
(i) Real golden questions created from Algorithm \ref{alg:high_certainty_selection}; and
(ii) Instructed questions, inspired by Instructed Manipulation Checks \cite{muszynski2023attention}, where an instruction would be added to assign a certain answer (e.g., ``Please select the first option''). However, their explicit instructional cue makes them less similar to non-golden questions and easier to identify (i.e., low ``quality''), in contrast to real golden questions. Please see Appendix \ref{subapx:field_exp} for the setup and more results.


