\textbf{Results.} Figure~\ref{fig:field_exp} presents the results. In Figure~\ref{fig:field_exp}(a), we define the "Correct" group as annotators who answered all golden questions correctly, and the "Incorrect" group as those who made at least one incorrect response. We use accuracy on non-golden questions as a proxy for overall annotation quality. As shown in Figure~\ref{fig:field_exp}(b), the real golden questions yield a larger accuracy gap between the Correct and Incorrect groups, suggesting they exert a stronger influence on annotator behavior than the instructed ones. Specifically, in Figure~\ref{fig:field_exp}(a),  annotators in the Correct group demonstrate higher annotation quality, and those in the Incorrect group perform worse. While the method of Instructed Questions has been a widely adopted method in social surveys, this experiment indicates that real golden questions are more effective at distinguishing high-quality annotators in the context of LLM data annotation. Consequently, these high-performing annotators could be reliably identified and potentially incentivized to produce even higher-quality annotations -- beyond what is achievable using instructed golden questions.


\section{Discussions and Conclusion}

In this paper, we formulate and study the problem of incentivizing high-quality human annotation. We extend the existing LLM post-training frameworks with a behavioral element and propose an MLE-based approach to monitor the performance of human annotators using a golden question dataset. We analyze the design of this annotation system under the classic principal-agent framework, and develop a new analysis approach for analyzing the gap between first-best and second-best solutions. The analysis approach augments the existing results on the principal-agent model and provides a new route for designing the monitoring hypothesis for the principal. In addition to these technical improvements, we want to emphasize our contribution in alerting the community to the issue of data annotation quality. Future works can be done on revisiting the current post-training alignments from our proposed behavioral models, and on a more fine-grained analysis of the human annotators' behavior patterns; for instance, which characteristics of the annotators make them less committed to which type of samples. Also, we believe our current analyses and results generalize to other formats of data annotation, such as ranking, but it is interesting to see the extent of such generalization and how much it applies to more complicated annotation tasks for multimodal LLMs and reasoning tasks. 


