\section{Related Literature}

\label{app:related_lit}

\subsection{Annotation monitoring and management}

Effective monitoring mechanisms help ensure annotators produce high-quality data, especially crucial for reinforcement learning from human feedback (RLHF) and direct preference optimization (DPO). RLHF has become a primary method for aligning large language models (LLMs) with human preferences. This approach trains a reward model using preference data and then fine-tunes the model to generate preferred responses \citep{askell2021general, ouyang2022training, ziegler2019fine}. For a comprehensive overview, see \cite{kaufmann2023survey}.

High-quality preference data is essential for successful RLHF. Recent research highlights that noisy data significantly reduces alignment performance \citep{gao2024impact, chowdhury2024provably, wang2024secrets}. Noise in preference annotations is common, often exceeding 20\% in real datasets \citep{zhao2023slic, munos2023nash, cui2023ultrafeedback, touvron2023llama}. Researchers have proposed various strategies to address this, such as label smoothing \citep{wang2024secrets}, filtering out noisy annotations \citep{gao2024impact, liang2024robust}, and robust loss functions \citep{gao2024impact, wang2024secrets, liang2024robust}. A recent approach, K-Free Nearest Neighbor \citep{zhang2024kfnn}, adaptively selects neighborhoods for more accurate label aggregation. Unlike these post-annotation solutions, our paper focuses on proactively incentivizing annotators to deliver better data quality.

Data quality management during annotation involves estimating and improving annotation accuracy. Quality estimation methods include AI-assisted monitoring \citep{li2024labelaid,pustejovsky2012natural, qian2021annotation, northcutt2021confident, klie2024efficient, ghosal2022cicero}, inserting known-answer tasks \citep{callison2010creating}, measuring inter-annotator agreement \citep{krippendorff1989content, krippendorff2004reliability, artstein2008inter, monarch2021human}, and using comparative contracts among annotators \citep{miller2005eliciting, bacon2012predicting, cai2015optimum, dasgupta2013crowdsourced}. Benchmark frameworks also exist to standardize data quality improvement practices \citep{goswami2023aqua}. If annotations fail quality standards, improvement strategies include retraining annotators \citep{bareket2021neural, klie2024analyzing, ghosal2022cicero} or filtering data \citep{bastan2020author}. See surveys \cite{klie2024analyzing, daniel2018quality} for more details.

However, preference data annotation faces two unique challenges. First, annotator heterogeneity makes traditional quality assessment difficult due to a lack of ground-truth labels. Second, the unclear link between annotation quality and downstream model performance complicates evaluation. These issues limit the effectiveness of traditional methods for preference data annotation.

\subsection{Contract design}

Contract design addresses how to motivate agents (annotators) to meet the principal's (company's) goals when information is asymmetric. This method is widely applied in healthcare and outsourcing \citep{de2019designing, corbett1999designing, adida2019contracts, jain2013optimal}. It also appears in machine learning contexts, such as verifying model accuracy \citep{goldwasser2021interactive}, aligning agents and principals in reinforcement learning \citep{ivanov2024principal}, and pricing annotated data in data markets \citep{agarwal2019marketplace, chen2022selling, ho2014adaptive, acemoglu2022too,bergemann2019markets}.

Recent studies propose simple yet effective contracts to manage machine learning delegation problems, where data quantity indicates agent effort and accuracy of resulted machine learning models indicates the principal utility (and is used to design contracts) \citep{ananthakrishnan2024delegating, saig2024delegated}. In contrast, our paper explicitly focuses on annotation quality as a measure of agent effort, examining how contract structures influence principal's utility.

In the context of LLMs, various mechanisms have been proposed to handle incentive-related issues, such as online learning mechanism for strategic annotators in RLHF \cite{hao2024online}, algorithmic contract design for enhancing the generated content's quality from LLMs \citep{saig2024incentivizing}, incentive-compatible auctions to aggregate multiple outputs from LLMs for advertisement \citep{duetting2024mechanism},   Bayesian persuasion framework to simulate receiver behavior based on generative AI \citep{harris2023algorithmic}, mechanisms for fine-tuning LLMs using aggregated reward models \citep{sun2024mechanism}. 

A substantial body of literature explores theoretical aspects of contract design \citep{dutting2021complexity,dutting2019simple,alon2022bayesian,10.1145/3670865.3673607}, with surveys available in \cite{dutting2024algorithmic,NBERw13480}. Within this research domain, our work aligns closely with studies examining optimality in linear and binary contracts. Specifically, analyses of binary contracts, including \cite{holmstrom1987aggregation,herweg2010binary,georgiadis2020optimal,lopomo2011knightian}, focus on contexts where binary contracts prove optimal. Concurrently, the literature \cite{holmstrom1987aggregation,walton2022general,carroll2015robustness,barron2020optimal}, explores linear frameworks. Diverging from these existing studies, our analysis primarily investigates the convergence rates toward the first-best solution in both binary and linear contract scenarios rather than examining optimality itself.

The closest related papers to our study are \cite{frick2023monitoring,liu2025humans}, which analyze how the principal's payoff approaches the first-best solution as the data volume grows, thereby progressively revealing the agent's effort. Unlike these works, which restrict their analysis to discrete annotation or label spaces, we extend our examination to maximum likelihood estimators (MLE) capable of accommodating continuous annotation spaces.

\subsection{Golden questions}

Golden questions (or gold standard questions) effectively validate annotation quality and are commonly used on platforms like Amazon Mechanical Turk \citep{le2010ensuring}. The literature examines their effectiveness from various perspectives. For instance, \cite{huang2013enhancing} compares golden questions and peer-consistency checks, \citep{harris2011you} finds positive incentives based on these questions improve worker accuracy. However, the golden question mechanism can be vulnerable to collusion among annotators \citep{checco2018all}. \citep{shah2016double} suggests payment systems encouraging workers to only answer confident questions and skip others based on golden questions.  \citep{oleson2011programmatic} proposes automating gold set creation (programmatic gold) for scalability based on previous annotated data with manual audits to decide the data mutations when creating new golden questions. An analogous techniques termed ``attention checks,'' are utilized in the field of survey design to gauge whether respondents are maintaining an adequate level of attention \citep{silber2022issue}; for an in-depth review of this specific area, \cite{muszynski2023attention} provide a thorough overview. Our work extends the literature by proposing an efficient method to select highly certain preference questions as golden questions, enhancing incentive alignment for annotators in large language model contexts. 

\section{Proofs and Discussions}

\subsection{Discussions on $\theta_a(F_n) = \theta^\ast$}
\label{subsec:proof_converge_to_optimal_theta}
To help the discussions, we define a more restricted definition of second-best solutions by adding one more constraint such that $\theta_a(F_n) = \theta^\ast$. Such a practice follows the literature \citep{frick2023monitoring, liu2025humans}, requiring $\theta_a(F_n) = \theta^\ast$ (recall that $\theta^\ast$ is the optimal solution to the first-best \eqref{eq:first_best}).
\begin{equation}
\begin{split}
\tilde{\mathcal{C}}_n \coloneqq \max_{F_n} \, & \mathbb{E}\left[U_p(w_a, \theta_a(F_n))\right], \\
\text{s.t. } & \mathbb{E}\left[U_a(w_a, \theta_a(F_n))\right] \geq U_0, \\
& \theta_a(F_n) \in \argmax_{\theta \in \Theta} \mathbb{E}\left[U_a(w_a, \theta)\right], \\
& \theta_a(F_n) = \theta^\ast.
\end{split}
\label{eq:restricted_second_best}
\end{equation}

For the three programs \eqref{eq:first_best}, \eqref{eq:second_best}, and \eqref{eq:restricted_second_best}, since the constraints are imposed one after another, we know 
\begin{equation*}
%\label{eqn:snd_best_rank}
\mathcal{C} \geq \mathcal{C}_n \geq \tilde{\mathcal{C}}_n.
\end{equation*}
Therefore, $\mathcal{C} - \tilde{\mathcal{C}}_n$ gives an upper bound for $\mathcal{C} - \mathcal{C}_n$.
At the same time, in the mini-max lower bound example of Theorem \ref{thm:binary}, $\mathcal{C} - \mathcal{C}_n$ is of the same order as $\mathcal{C} - \tilde{\mathcal{C}}_n$ when $n$ grows large. In that sense, the restricted second-best gives us a handle to control that $\theta_a(F_n) = \theta^\ast$ when analyzing the second-best, setting up a benchmark for the variance.


Once we prove the gap of $\mathcal{C} - \tilde{\mathcal{C}}_n = \mathcal{O}(1/\sqrt{n \log n})$, one can also prove that as $n$ grows larger, the second-best action $\theta_a(F_n^\ast)$ will converge to $\theta^\ast$ with a rate
\[
\| \theta_a(F_n^\ast) - \theta^\ast\|^2 = \mathcal{O}\left(1/\sqrt{n \log n}\right)
\]
by using the strong convexity of the target function. That proof is analogous to \cite{liu2025humans}, which we present for completeness here.

\begin{proposition}
\label{prop:second_converge_to_first}
Denote the optimal solution to \eqref{eq:second_best} by $\theta_a(F_n^\ast)$. Then, under the condition of Theorem \ref{thm:binary} and further assuming the strong convexity of $G_a^{-1}(E(\theta) + u) - \mu(\theta)$ as a function of $\theta$, if we have $\mathcal{C} - \tilde{\mathcal{C}}_n = \mathcal{O}(1/\sqrt{n \log n})$,
then
\[
\left\|\theta_a(F_n^\ast) - \theta^\ast\right\|^2 = \mathcal{O}\left(1/\sqrt{n \log n}\right).
\]
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}[Proof of Proposition \ref{prop:second_converge_to_first}]
For any $\theta \in \Theta$, define two optimization problems in analogy to the first-best/second-best problems:
\begin{equation}
\begin{split}
C_{\text{first}}(\theta) \coloneqq \max_{F_n} \ &\mathbb{E}[U_p(w_a, \theta)]\\
\text{s.t. }& \mathbb{E}[U_a(w_a, \theta)]\geq U_0. \quad \text{(IR)}
\end{split}
\label{eq:first_best_auxilliary}
\end{equation}
The above one is to analyze the principal's first-best payoff if the agent takes action $\theta$.
\begin{equation}
\begin{split}
C_{\text{second}}(\theta) \coloneqq \max_{F_n} \ &\mathbb{E}[U_p(w_a, \theta)],\\
\text{s.t. }& \mathbb{E}[U_a(w_a, \theta)]\geq U_0, \quad \text{(IR)}\\
& \mathbb{E}[U_a(w_a, \theta)]\geq \sup_{\theta^\prime \in \Theta} \mathbb{E}[U_a(w_a, \theta^\prime)]. \quad \text{(IC)}
\end{split}
\label{eq:second_best_auxilliary}
\end{equation}
The above one is to analyze the principal's first-best payoff if the agent takes action $\theta$.
Denote the optimal second-best solution by $F_n^\ast$. Note that the maximum to the first-best problem \eqref{eq:first_best} equals $C_{\text{first}}(\theta^\ast)$. Also, the maximum to the second-best problem \eqref{eq:second_best} equals $C_{\text{second}}(\theta_a(F_n^\ast))$ and the maximum to the restricted second-best problem \eqref{eq:restricted_second_best} equals $C_{\text{second}}(\theta^\ast)$. We have
\begin{align*}
C_{\text{second}}(\theta_a(F_n^\ast)) & \geq C_{\text{first}}(\theta_a(F_n^\ast))\\
& \geq C_{\text{first}}(\theta^\ast)\\
& \geq C_{\text{second}}(\theta^\ast) - \Theta\left(1/\sqrt{n \log n}\right).
\end{align*}
Here, the first inequality is due to \eqref{eq:first_best_auxilliary}'s removing the IC constraint can only induce an optimum no smaller than \eqref{eq:second_best_auxilliary}, the second inequality is due to the optimality of $\theta_a(F_n^\ast)$ subject to the original second-best problem \eqref{eq:second_best}, and the last from the proposition's assumption.


Now, since the first-best problem must have a binding constraint (otherwise, the principal can always reduce the wage to get a better utility while still satisfying the IR constraint), we have
\[
\mu(\theta_a(F_n^\ast)) - G_a^{-1}(U_0 + E(\theta_a(F_n^\ast))) \geq \mu(\theta^\ast) - G_a^{-1}(U_0 + E(\theta^\ast)) - \Theta\left(1/\sqrt{n \log n}\right).
\]
From the strong convexity, we have
\[
\left(\theta_a(F_n^\ast) - \theta^\ast\right)^2 = \mathcal{O}\left(1/\sqrt{n \log n}\right).
\]
\end{proof}


Therefore, it does no harm to think of $\theta^\ast$ as a proxy of $\theta_a(F_n^\ast)$ when $n$ is large.


\subsection{Proof of Theorem \ref{thm:binary}}
\label{subsec:proof_main_theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof of the upper bound part]
We are considering the MLE estimator
\[
\hat{\theta} \in \argmax_{\theta \in \Theta} \sum_{j=1}^n \log (p(d_j; \theta)),
\]
where
\[
d_j \overset{\text{i.i.d.}}{\sim} p(\cdot; \theta_a).
\]
Without loss of generality, we restrict our attention to the 1-dimensional case where $\theta \subset \mathbb{R}$; otherwise, we can always analyze any $\theta^\ast + t v$ for any unit vector $v$ along the line $t \in \mathbb{R}$ to get the same result.


Following the notational convention in the $M$-estimator analysis (or the $Z$-estimator), we denote
\[
m_j(\theta) \coloneqq \log (p(d_j ; \theta)), \quad M(\theta) \coloneqq \frac1n\sum_{j=1}^n m_j(\theta),
\]
\[
z_j(\theta) \coloneqq \nabla_{\theta} \, m_j(\theta), \quad Z(\theta) \coloneqq \frac1n \sum_{j=1}^n z_j(\theta).
\]
Since we assume the strong concavity of $m_j(\theta)$, the maximum of $M(\theta)$ must be uniquely obtained at $\hat{\theta}$. Equivalently, $\hat{\theta}$ is also the unique zero point of $Z(\theta)$, where $Z(\theta)$ is a monotonically decreasing function.


We only focus on a subclass of the binary contract, where the reject region is characterized by a parameter $\tau \in \Theta \subset \mathbb{R}$, where
\[
\mathcal{R}_n(\tau) \coloneqq \{\hat{\theta} < \tau\}.
\]
If we can find some $\tau$ such that the variance of the payment is of $\mathcal{O}\big(1 / \sqrt{n \log n}\big)$, then the upper bound claim will be proved. The reject region can be translated into that of $Z(\theta)$:
\[
\mathcal{R}_n(\tau) = \{Z(\tau) < 0\}.
\]


To calculate the relation between $\tau$ and $\theta_a$, we need to specify the impact of $\tau$ on the choice of $\theta_a$ when the agent is choosing $\theta_a$ to maximize $\mathbb{E}[w_a] - E(\theta_a)$. We want to calculate the first-order condition (FOC), where
\[
\nabla_{\theta_a} \, \mathbb{E}[w_a] - \nabla_{\theta_a} E(\theta_a) = 0.
\]
The incentive (or the marginal utility) of the agent to increase the annotation quality (all conditioned on the distribution of each $d_j$ is $p(\cdot; \theta_a)$, where we omit for notational simplicity) is
\allowdisplaybreaks
\begin{align*}
I(\theta_a) & \coloneqq \nabla_{\theta_a} \, \mathbb{E}[w_a] \\
   & = W_b \cdot \nabla_{\theta_a} \, \mathbb{P}(Z(\tau) \geq 0) \\
   & = W_b \cdot \nabla_{\theta_a} \, \int_{d_1, \dots, d_n, Z(\tau) \geq 0} \prod_{j=1}^n \, p(d_j ; \theta_a) \, \mathrm{d} \lambda \\
   & = W_b \cdot \int_{d_1, \dots, d_n, Z(\tau) \geq 0} \left(\sum_{j=1}^n \nabla_{\theta_a}\, p(d_j ; \theta_a) \cdot \prod_{1\leq k \leq n, k \neq j} p(d_k ; \theta_a) \right) \mathrm{d} \lambda \\
   & = W_b \cdot \int_{d_1, \dots, d_n, Z(\tau) \geq 0} \left(\sum_{j=1}^n \nabla_{\theta_a}\, \log(p(d_j ; \theta_a)) \cdot \prod_{k=1}^n p(d_k ; \theta_a) \right) \mathrm{d} \lambda \\
   & = W_b \cdot \int_{d_1, \dots, d_n, Z(\tau) \geq 0} n \cdot Z(\theta_a) \cdot \prod_{j=1}^n p(d_j ; \theta_a) \mathrm{d} \lambda\\
   & = W_b \cdot \sqrt{n\mathrm{Var}(z_1(\theta_a))} \cdot \int_{Z(\tau) \geq 0} \frac{\sum_{j=1}^n z_j(\theta_a)}{\sqrt{n\mathrm{Var}(z_1(\theta_a))}} \cdot \prod_{j=1}^n p(d_j ; \theta_a) \mathrm{d} \lambda.
\end{align*}
Here, $\lambda$ is the Lebesgue measure of $(d_1, \dots, d_n) \in \mathcal{D}^n$. The first equality is due to the definition of $w_a$, the second is writing the probability in the integration form, the third is changing the order of integration and derivation (it is feasible as the dominated convergence theorem shows us in the textbook), the fourth is the ``log-trick'' where $\nabla_\theta \log (p(x)) = \nabla_\theta (p(\theta)) / p(\theta)$, the fifth is because of the definition of $Z$, and the sixth is reorganizing the expression. 


% Here we want to correct a typo in the main text of our NeurIPS main paper submission. In Theorem \ref{thm:binary}, the upper bound should be $\mathcal{O}(1/\sqrt{n \log n})$, just as the abstract and the lower bound. 
Now the upper bound has been proved for this normal distribution example. Without loss of generality, we can assume that $\mathrm{Var}(z_1(\theta_a)) = 1$ to ease the notation in the following steps.


Now, as a high-level intuition, the quantity inside the integration will converge to the standard normal distribution by the central limit theorem:
\[
\frac{\sum_{j=1}^n z_j(\theta_a)}{\sqrt{n}} \xrightarrow[]{d} \mathcal{N}(0, 1),
\]
since each $z_j(\theta_a)$ is i.i.d., mean-zero, and of finite variance (where we have assumed to be 1 to ease the notation). We denote the normalized average by
\[
\tilde{Z}(\theta_a) \coloneqq \frac{\sum_{j=1}^n z_j(\theta_a)}{\sqrt{n}}.
\]
Then the incentive can be written as
\begin{equation}
    I(\theta_a) = W_b \cdot \sqrt{n} \cdot \int_{Z(\tau) \geq 0} \tilde{Z}(\theta_a) \, \mathrm{d} \mathcal{P}(\tilde{Z}(\theta_a)),
\end{equation}
where $\mathcal{P}(\tilde{Z}(\theta_a))$ denotes the distribution of $\tilde{Z}(\theta_a)$.


To obtain a good intuition of the problem structure, we start from the normal distribution where each $d_j$ is a real-valued score, $p(d_j; \theta_a) = \frac{1}{\sqrt{2 \pi}} \cdot \exp(-\frac{(d_j - \theta_a)^2}{2})$, and $z_j(\theta_a) = d_j - \theta_a \sim \mathcal{N}(0, 1)$ (just as the SFT data annotation example with $\sigma^2 = 1$ in Section \ref{sec:setup}). In that case, the quantity is already normally distributed:
\[
\tilde{Z}(\theta_a) \sim \mathcal{N}(0, 1),
\]
and the integration constraint $Z(\tau) \geq 0$ is equivalent to
\[
\tilde{Z}(\theta_a) \geq \sqrt{n} \cdot (\tau - \theta_a).
\]
Then the incentive is:
\begin{align*}
I(\theta_a) & = W_b \cdot \sqrt{n} \cdot \int_{\tilde{Z}(\theta_a) \geq \sqrt{n} \cdot (\tau - \theta_a)} \tilde{Z}(\theta_a) \, \mathrm{d} \mathcal{P}(\tilde{Z}(\theta_a)) \\
& = W_b \cdot \sqrt{n} \cdot \int_{\sqrt{n} \cdot (\tau - \theta_a)}^{\infty} x \cdot \frac{1}{\sqrt{2 \pi}} \cdot \exp\left(-\frac{x^2}{2}\right) \mathrm{d} x \\
& = W_b \cdot \frac{\sqrt{n}}{\sqrt{2 \pi}} \cdot \exp\left(-\frac{n (\theta_a - \tau)^2}{2}\right).
\end{align*}
The above calculations show that the incentive should be just as ($W_b$ times) the p.d.f. of a normal distribution centered at $\tau$ and is of $1/n$ variance. Now, the first-order condition is met at some 
\[
( \theta_a - \tau )^2 = \frac{2}{n} \cdot \left(\frac12 \log n + \log(W_b) - \frac12\log(2\pi) - \log\big(E(\theta_a)\big)\right).
\]
That is because: (1) the derivative $\nabla_{\theta_a}E(\theta_a)$ is positive at $\theta_a = \theta^\ast$ (because we have assumed that at $\theta^\ast$, $E$ is not minimized); and (2) the derivative $\nabla_{\theta_a}E(\theta_a)$ is at a constant level independent of $n$. Solving the FOC gives us the above claim. As $n$ grows large, the $I(\theta_a)$ becomes the dominant term in deciding the sign of the first-order derivative. Hence, we can conclude that the maximizing point must be taken at some $\theta_a > \tau$:
\[
\theta_a - \tau = \left(\frac{2}{n} \cdot \left(\frac12 \log n + \log(W_b) - \frac12\log(2\pi) - \log\big(E(\theta_a)\big)\right)\right)^{\frac12}.
\]


Now we calculate the variance of the test $\mathrm{Var}(\Psi)$. The probability that the agent fails the test is
\begin{align*}
    \mathbb{P}(\Psi = 0) & = \int_{-\infty}^{\sqrt{n} \cdot (\tau - \theta_a)} \frac{1}{\sqrt{2 \pi}} \cdot \exp\left(-\frac{x^2}{2}\right) \mathrm{d}x\\
    & = 1 - \Phi\left(\sqrt{n} \cdot (\theta_a - \tau)\right),
\end{align*}
where $\Phi(x)$ is the c.d.f. of the standard normal distribution. We denote the p.d.f. of the standard normal distribution by $\phi(x)$. By approximation scheme $1/x - 1/x^3\leq\frac{1 - \Phi(x)}{\phi(x)} \leq 1/x$, we shall see that
\begin{align*}
    \mathbb{P}(\Psi = 0) & = \Theta\left(\phi\left(\sqrt{n} \cdot (\theta_a - \tau)\right) \cdot \frac{1}{\sqrt{n} \cdot (\theta_a - \tau)} \right) \\
    & = \Theta\left(\frac{1}{\sqrt{n \log n}}\right).
\end{align*}



For general distributions, the target is to show that, as $\tilde{Z}(\theta_a)$ converges to the standard normal distribution, the above arguments still hold. The most important property is the \emph{convergence rate} of $\tilde{Z}(\theta_a)$ to $\mathcal{N}(0, 1)$, where we adopt the Berry-Esseen type bounds to achieve so. Since we have assumed the strong-convexity and smoothness of the negative log-likelihood (each $-m_j(\theta)$), the derivative of $m_j(\theta)$ (which is $z_j(\theta)$) can be approximated by a linear function. That is to say, we have some $\gamma \in (\alpha, \beta)$, such that $\int_{Z(\tau) \geq 0} \prod_{j=1}^n p(d_j; \theta_a) \mathrm{d} \lambda = \int_{Z(\theta_a) \geq \gamma \cdot (\tau - \theta_a)} \prod_{j=1}^n p(d_j; \theta_a) \mathrm{d} \lambda$. In that case, the proof is totally the same, while there are only two things yet to be proved (both related to the convergence rate of $\tilde{Z}(\theta_a)$ to $\mathcal{N}(0, 1)$):
\begin{enumerate}
    \item The partial moment of $\tilde{Z}(\theta_a)$ converges to that of the standard normal distribution at a rate of $\mathcal{O}\big(1/\sqrt{n}\big)$;
    \item The probability of $\Psi = 0$ as some c.d.f. of $\tilde{Z}(\theta_a)$ at some FOC converges to the c.d.f. of the standard normal distribution at a rate of $\mathcal{O}\big(1/\sqrt{n \log n}\big)$.
\end{enumerate}


For the first claim, by integration by parts, we have (assuming the c.d.f. of $\mathcal{P}(\tilde{Z}(\theta_a))$ by $F_n$):
\[
    \int_{C}^\infty \tilde{Z}(\theta_a)\, \mathrm{d} \mathcal{P}(\tilde{Z}(\theta_a)) = - C F_n(C) - \int_{C}^{\infty} F_n(z) \, \mathrm{d}z,
\]
due to that $\tilde{Z}(\theta_a)$ is of finite first-moment (which is guaranteed by the assumption that $\tilde{Z}(\theta_a)$ is of finite third-moment).

Applying the non-uniform version of Berry-Esseen type convergence bounds \citep{nagaev1965some, michel1981constant}, we have that under the condition of Assumption \ref{assum:regulating_likelihood} (a),
\[
|F_n(z) - \Phi(z) | \leq \frac{C_1}{1+|z|^3} \cdot \frac{1}{\sqrt{n}}, \quad \text{ for some constant } C_1.
\]
The above argument suggests that
\[
|C \Phi(C) - C F_n(C) | \leq \frac{|C|}{1+|C|^3} \cdot \frac{C_1}{\sqrt{n}} = \mathcal{O}\big(1/\sqrt{n} \big),
\]
and
\[
\left|\int_{C}^\infty \big( F_n(z) - \Phi(z) \big) \, \mathrm{d}z \right| \leq \frac{C_1}{\sqrt{n}} \cdot \int_{-\infty}^\infty \frac{1}{1+|z|^3} \, \mathrm{d} z = \mathcal{O}\big(1/\sqrt{n} \big),
\]
which verifies the first claim. In that sense, the incentive $I(\theta_a)$ is exactly
\[
I(\theta_a) = W_b \cdot \frac{\sqrt{n}}{\sqrt{2 \pi}} \cdot \exp\left(-\frac{n (\theta_a - \tau)^2}{2}\right) + \mathcal{O}(1).
\]
Now the intersection of $I(\theta_a)$ and $\nabla_{\theta_a} E(\theta_a)$ (which is the FOC) is of the same scale as the normal distribution's case, since $\nabla_{\theta_a}E(\theta_a)$ itself is $\Theta(1)$. That is to say,
\[
\theta_a - \tau = \left(\frac{2}{n} \cdot \left(\frac12 \log n + \log(W_b) - \frac12\log(2\pi) - \log\big(E(\theta_a) + \mathcal{O}(1)\big)\right)\right)^{\frac12}.
\]


Now we have the same intuition as the normal case that $\sqrt{n} \cdot (\theta_a - \tau) = \Theta\big(\sqrt{\log n}\big)$. We turn to prove the second claim. Using the non-uniform Berry-Esseen bound \citep{nagaev1965some, michel1981constant} once again, we have
\begin{align*}
    \mathbb{P}(\Psi = 0) & = F_n\big(\sqrt{n} \cdot (\tau - \theta_a)\big) \\
    & = \Phi\big(\sqrt{n} \cdot (\tau - \theta_a)\big) + \mathcal{O}\left(\frac{1}{\sqrt{n}} \cdot \frac{1}{1+(\log n)^{\frac32}}\right)\\
    & = \Phi\big(\sqrt{n} \cdot (\tau - \theta_a)\big) + \mathcal{O}\big(1/\sqrt{n \log n}\big),
\end{align*}
where we can adopt the same estimation on $\Phi\big(\sqrt{n} \cdot (\tau - \theta_a)\big) = 1 - \Phi\big(\sqrt{n} \cdot (\theta_a - \tau)\big)$ as the normal distribution's case. Concluding the proof, we note that
\[
\mathbb{P}(\Psi = 0) = \Theta\left(\frac{1}{\sqrt{n \log n}}\right),
\]
for general MLE estimators under Assumptions \ref{assum:regular_PA_model} and \ref{assum:regulating_likelihood}.
As a consequence, the variance is of the same scale as $\mathbb{P}(\Psi = 0)$ since $\mathbb{P}(\Psi = 1)$ is approximately 1.
\end{proof}

\begin{proof}[Proof of the lower bound part]
    The lower bound is a direct corollary of the lower bound part of Theorem 4.6 of \cite{liu2025humans}, noticing that the sample average of a binomial distribution is the MLE. In that sense, the lower bound in \cite{liu2025humans} provides a mini-max lower bound for the MLE hypothesis testing under the principal-agent model.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Further Discussions on Implications of Our Theory}
\label{subsec:further_implications}
We have already listed two criteria for designing golden questions in Section \ref{sec:experi}. We recall that a good set of golden questions should be: (1) of sufficiently certain answers; and (2) of a similar format to the other questions. Our model assumptions can explain both: we have assumed in \eqref{eqn:monitoring_data} that: (1) the monitoring data $d_j$'s are i.i.d. according to $p(\cdot\,; \theta_a)$; and (2) the monitoring data share the same $\theta_a$ as the other data in $\bar{\mathcal{S}}_N$. In Section \ref{sec:theory}, we use the population-level model \eqref{eqn:annotation_model} to represent the behavior of a certain annotator. That is not a big problem if the population has a consistent answer to the golden questions $\mathcal{D}_n$. However, if the golden questions' answers are uncertain, different annotators may have very different opinions, violating the assumption that all the behaviors of the annotator can be modeled by the population-level model. Besides, in a proper likelihood model, the likelihood should always be more biased towards the ``correct'' answer if the annotator spends more effort. But it is very hard to define what a ``correct'' answer is for those controversial questions, bringing up additional difficulties in modeling the annotators' behavior using the likelihood approach. As for the format of golden questions, we need to avoid cases where the annotator can easily tell the golden questions $\mathcal{D}_n$ from the normal ones such that the agent (the annotator) can cheat the system by paying efforts only to $\mathcal{D}_n$ rather than the entire dataset $\bar{\mathcal{S}}_N$. In those situations, the assumption \eqref{eqn:monitoring_data} is also violated because $\mathcal{D}_n$ is not generated by the same $\theta_a$ anymore.


\subsection{Discussions on other contracts}
\label{subsec:linear_contract}

One of the most straightforward solutions to reducing $\mathrm{Var}(w_a)$ is to use $n$ samples' average outcome, which is at the rate $\Theta(1/n)$. Such an intuition is formalized as the linear contract in this subsection.


Under linear contracts, each contract $F_n$ is specified by a function $f_n$ that maps from each monitoring sample $d_j$ in $\mathcal{D}_n$ to a positive value and pays the agent the average of $f_n(d_j)$. The procedure is described by Algorithm \ref{alg_lin_contr}. Then the payment level is $w_a = \frac{1}{n} \sum_{j=1}^d f_n(d_j)$. We denote all those contracts by $\mathcal{F}_n^{\text{lin}}$, and the corresponding second-best values within $\mathcal{F}_n^{\text{lin}}$ by $\mathcal{C}_n^{\text{lin}}$.

\begin{algorithm}[ht!]
\centering
\caption{Linear contract}
\label{alg_lin_contr}
\begin{algorithmic}
\State \textbf{Input:} A dataset $\mathcal{D}_n=\{d_1,...,d_n\}$ used to assess the annotator performance and a linear contract $F_n$ specified by $f_n$
% \State \textcolor{blue}{\%\% $f_n(d_j)\in \mathbb{R}$ maps each assessment data sample $d_j$ to a payment amount (say, based on its quality)}
\State  The company pays the annotator $$w_a = \frac1n \sum_{i=1}^n f_n(d_j)$$
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\begin{proposition}
\label{prop:linear_contract}
Under Assumptions \ref{assum:regular_PA_model}, we have
\[
\mathcal{C} - \mathcal{C}_n^{\text{lin}} = \Theta\left(\frac{1}{n}\right).
\]
\end{proposition}

Proposition \ref{thm:linear_contract} gives a faster rate between the first-best and second-best solutions: the company is averaging the outcomes using $n$ samples. The analysis here is standard, following \cite{liu2025humans}, where we omit for simplicity. From the upper bounds, we shall see that, similar to the binary contract in the main paper, in order to obtain a smaller variance on the outcome, we need to increase $n$.


Another thing to note is that, although those linear contracts are of a better rate with respect to $n$, linear contracts lack clear control of the agent's action. If we set the binary contract to reject $\hat{\theta}$ by setting a standard like $\|\hat{\theta} - \theta^\ast\| > \tau$, then we can guarantee that the agent's action must fall within $\|\theta_a(F_n) - \theta^\ast\| \leq \tau$ as is shown in the proof of Theorem \ref{thm:binary} in Appendix \ref{subsec:proof_main_theorem}. However, in the linear contract case, we do not have such a guarantee of the agent's action. In that sense, the linear contract is less clear than the binary contract.

\section{Appendix for Numerical Experiments}
\label{appx:experiments}
\subsection{Setup and More Results for Figure \ref{fig:Accuracy_SkyGemma}}
\label{subapx:data_describ_extensions}

In Algorithm \ref{alg:high_certainty_selection}, we present our automatic generation algorithm for the gold questions. It relies on a certainty estimator $c(\cdot)$ which, for each sample $s_i$, estimates the certainty of its answer. For the preference dataset where $s=(x,y_1,y_2)$, we may obtain $c(s)$ from a pretrained reward model~$r(\cdot)$. Specifically, the model estimates the probability that $y_1$ is preferred over $y_2$ as
\[
\widehat p_r(x,y_1,y_2)
\;=\;
\dfrac{1}{1 + \exp\bigl(r(x,y_2) - r(x,y_1)\bigr)}.
\]
We then define the certainty estimator as
\[
c(x,y_1,y_2) \;=\; 2\cdot\bigl|\,1/2 - \widehat p_r(x,y_1,y_2)\bigr|.
\]
Thus $c(s)\approx1$ when the model is confident (i.e.\ $\widehat p_r$ near 0 or 1) and $c(s)\approx0$ when it is maximally uncertain ($\widehat p_r\approx0.5$). The experiment result in Figure \ref{fig:Accuracy_SkyGemma} validates that samples with high certainty imply that they are non-ambiguous and that their real answers can be correctly predicted. Concretely, we test the accuracy of the reward models on six human preference datasets. For each dataset, we select its test or validation split to evaluate the accuracy of the reward model. The detailed description of the datasets and their respective data processing are given below:

\begin{enumerate}

\item \href{https://huggingface.co/datasets/openai/summarize_from_feedback}{\textbf{Summarize:}} The OpenAI's \textbf{Summarize from Feedback} \cite{stiennon2020learning} dataset contains human annotations for evaluating summaries, organized into two main subsets: comparisons (preference-based pairwise selections) and axis scores (multi-dimensional quality ratings such as coherence, accuracy, and coverage). The data sources include Reddit posts (TL;DR dataset), CNN articles, and Daily Mail articles. We select the validation split of the comparisons subset to evaluate the accuracy of the reward models.

\item \href{https://huggingface.co/datasets/Anthropic/hh-rlhf}{\textbf{HHRlhf:}} The \textbf{HH-RLHF} \cite{bai2022training} dataset from Anthropic comprises preference pairs and red-teaming conversations. Annotators select preferred responses based on helpfulness and harmlessness criteria. The test split is selected for model evaluation.

\item \href{https://huggingface.co/datasets/PKU-Alignment/PKU-SafeRLHF}{\textbf{PKUSafe:}} The \textbf{PKU-SafeRLHF} \cite{ji2024beavertails} dataset is designed for training reward models that balance helpfulness and safety. It contains approximately 83.4K annotated question-answer pairs, with human preference annotations across helpfulness and harmlessness dimensions, complemented by detailed safety meta-labels categorizing potential harm types and severity. We select the test split and exclude samples where both responses are simultaneously safe or unsafe. For the remaining samples, the safe response is naturally preferred over the unsafe one.


\item \href{https://huggingface.co/datasets/HuggingFaceH4/ultrafeedback_binarized}{\textbf{UltraFeedback:}} The \textbf{UltraFeedback} \cite{cui2023ultrafeedback} (binarized) dataset consists of 64K prompts, with the replies generated by a wide variety of models and evaluated by GPT-4. Replies are binarized into "chosen" (highest GPT-4 rated reply) and "rejected" (randomly selected lower-rated reply), thus forming pairs suitable for preference-based training of reward models.

\item \href{https://huggingface.co/datasets/allenai/reward-bench}{\textbf{RewardBench:}} The \textbf{RewardBench} \cite{lambert2024rewardbench} dataset is designed to evaluate the reward models over their chatting, safety, and reasoning abilities. It integrates multiple preference-evaluation subsets spanning diverse domains such as chat dialogues (e.g., AlpacaEval, MT-Bench), safety tasks, and reasoning challenges (code/math problems). The filtered split is used in this experiment.

\item \href{https://huggingface.co/datasets/lmsys/chatbot_arena_conversations}{\textbf{Arena:}} The \textbf{Chatbot Arena Conversations} \cite{zheng2023judging} dataset features around 33K user queries paired with conversational replies from different language models. Human annotators provide preference votes between competing dialogues. We exclude the samples where the ``winner'' of the two responses says ``tie'' and test on the rest samples.

\end{enumerate}

We replicate the experiment from Figure \ref{fig:Accuracy_SkyGemma} using two additional reward models: \texttt{URM-LLaMa-3-8B} and \texttt{GRM-Llama3.2-3B}. All experiments are conducted on three NVIDIA A40 GPUs, with mixed-precision (FP16) acceleration. The results are presented in Figure \ref{fig:Accuracy_GRMLlama}. Despite variations in average accuracy across different models, their performance consistently aligns closely with human annotations on high-certainty samples. 



