\subsection{Setup and more results for Figure \ref{fig:field_exp}}
\label{subapx:field_exp}

\paragraph{Experiment setup}

We conduct our experiments using the PKU-SafeRLHF dataset \citep{ji2024pku}.

\textbf{Non-golden question construction.}
We randomly sample 7 preference data points from the testing set to serve as non-golden annotation tasks. To ensure the effectiveness in evaluating annotation quality, we only select samples for which the trained reward model \citep{dai2024safe} estimates a probability $\mathbb{P}(y_{\text{chosen}} \succ y_{\text{rejected}} \mid x) > 0.9$. Here, $y_{\text{chosen}}$ and $y_{\text{rejected}}$ represent the preferred and rejected responses in the human-annotated data provided in the dataset, respectively. This filtering ensures that selecting the previously preferred response provided in the dataset serves as a reliable measure of annotation quality.

\textbf{Golden question construction.}
To construct real golden questions, we apply Algorithm~\ref{alg:high_certainty_selection} using the reward model \textit{Skywork-Reward-Gemma-2-27B-v0.2} on the PKU-SafeRLHF testing set. We select the top 2 most certain samples, each with a model-estimated preference probability exceeding $0.9999$ according to the reward model \cite{ji2024beavertails} trained on this dataset, which demonstrates the effectiveness of the selection method.

For the instructed golden questions, we append an instruction such as:
``This is an attention check question. Please select Response 1 to receive the bonus.''
This instruction is added to the same two questions used as real golden questions, and the correct response (either ``Response 1'' or ``Response 2'') is randomly assigned. This approach minimizes any change to the question content aside from the added instruction.


\textbf{Payment and contract design.}
We conduct the annotation tasks on the Prolific platform (www.prolific.com), a widely used platform for human data collection. The task description is as follows:
``Choose the preferred response from two options. Each worker receives a fixed base payment plus a bonus. The bonus is awarded ONLY if you correctly answer the Attention Check Question (which looks similar to the others).'' The base payment is set at £1, with a £0.5 bonus. Prolific supports post-task bonus payments, allowing us to incentivize annotators based on their performance. This design provides a realistic and persuasive incentive structure, reinforcing the credibility of our findings. 

We randomly recruit 90 annotators through Prolific. Each annotator complete 9 annotation tasks, of which 2 are golden questions. Among them, 45 annotators receive instructed golden questions, while the remaining 45 receive real golden questions. Annotators will get bonus payment if they correctly answer all golden questions. The other 7 questions also have relatively clear correct answers.

In the annotation interface, we include the instruction:
``Please select the single response that is safer (i.e., less likely to harm or offend society),''
to clarify the evaluation criterion. All annotation tasks used in the experiment are attached at the end of this subsection.


\paragraph{Histogram of accuracy}\ Figure \ref{fig:hist_acc} illustrates how annotators’ performance on golden questions correlates with their overall annotation quality. In both instructed and real golden settings, annotators who correctly answered all golden questions (“Correct” group) achieved higher mean accuracy on non-golden questions than those who made errors (“Incorrect” group). The real golden question condition (bottom row) shows a larger performance gap between Correct and Incorrect groups (92.2\% vs. 81.1\%), compared to the instructed condition (90.3\% vs. 83.3\%). This suggests that real golden questions are more effective at distinguishing high-quality annotators and may better influence careful behavior.

